var tafsirData_5 = {
  "1": "'O believers! Honour your obligations. All grazing livestock has been made lawful to you—except what is hereby announced to you and hunting while on pilgrimage. Indeed, Allah commands what He wills.' (Quran 5:1). The surah opens with one word that covers everything.\n\n'Honour your obligations' — awfu bi al-'uqud. An ''aqd' is a knot, something tied. The plural is left completely open: no category is named, so every kind of binding agreement is included.\n\nThat covers the covenant with Allah, treaties between states, business contracts, employment terms, marriage — which 4:21 called 'a firm covenant' — and the ordinary promise you made to someone last week.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that the command covers what Allah has bound His servants to as well as what people bind themselves to among one another.\n\nThen food: 'All grazing livestock has been made lawful to you' — uhillat lakum bahimatu al-an'am. The default is permission. Islam's dietary law works by naming a short list of exceptions, not by requiring approval for each item.\n\nTwo exceptions are flagged immediately, and both are spelled out later in the surah: the list in 5:3, and hunting while in the state of ihram, detailed in 5:95.\n\n'Indeed, Allah commands what He wills' — inna Allaha yahkumu ma yurid. Placed here, this closes the door on negotiating the terms. A person who honours contracts with people but treats Allah's rulings as open to revision has misunderstood the first command in the verse.\n\nNotice the pairing that runs through the whole surah: a moral principle, then a concrete rule. Keep your word — and here is exactly what you may eat.",
  "2": "'O believers! Do not violate Allah's rituals ˹of pilgrimage˺, the sacred months, the sacrificial animals, the ˹offerings decorated with˺ garlands, nor those ˹pilgrims˺ on their way to the Sacred House seeking their Lord's bounty and pleasure. When pilgrimage has ended, you are allowed to hunt. Do not let the hatred of a people who once barred you from the Sacred Mosque provoke you to transgress. Cooperate with one another in goodness and righteousness, and do not cooperate in sin and transgression. And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is severe in punishment.' (Quran 5:2). A rule for people who have been wronged.\n\n'Allah's rituals' — sha'a'ir Allah. Visible markers of worship. The Quran says of them: 'whoever honours the symbols of Allah, it is certainly out of the piety of the heart' (Quran 22:32).\n\nThen the historical wound. 'A people who once barred you from the Sacred Mosque' refers to the Quraysh at Hudaybiyyah, where the Muslims arrived for 'umrah and were turned back. Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan reported the Prophet's words there: 'By the Name of Him in Whose Hands my soul is, if they ask me anything which will respect the ordinances of Allah, I will grant it to them' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2731).\n\n'Do not let the hatred of a people... provoke you to transgress' — wa la yajrimannakum shana'anu qawmin. Real grievance, genuinely suffered, and it still does not license retaliation against pilgrims travelling in peace.\n\n'Cooperate with one another in goodness and righteousness, and do not cooperate in sin and transgression' — wa ta'awanu 'ala al-birri wa al-taqwa. One of the most quoted lines in Islamic ethics, and it cuts both ways: you are responsible for what you help other people do.",
  "3": "'Forbidden to you are carrion, blood, and swine; what is slaughtered in the name of any other than Allah... Today I have perfected your faith for you, completed My favour upon you, and chosen Islam as your way. But whoever is compelled by extreme hunger—not intending to sin—then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 5:3). The full list, and the announcement in the middle of it.\n\nThe four basic prohibitions match 2:173, 6:145 and 16:115: carrion, flowing blood, pork, and anything dedicated to other than Allah. This verse adds the ways an animal may have died — strangled, beaten, fallen, gored, or partly eaten by a predator — and permits it if you reach it alive and slaughter it properly.\n\nThat detail matters. The concern is not only what the animal is, but how it died. Shaddad ibn Aws reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Verily Allah has enjoined goodness to everything; so when you kill, kill in a good way and when you slaughter, slaughter in a good way. So every one of you should sharpen his knife, and let the slaughtered animal die comfortably' (Sahih Muslim 1955a).\n\n'Today I have perfected your faith for you' — al-yawma akmaltu lakum dinakum. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab reported that a Jewish man said to him: 'There is a verse in your Holy Book which is read by all of you, and had it been revealed to us, we would have taken that day as a day of celebration.' 'Umar replied: 'No doubt, we know when and where this verse was revealed to the Prophet. It was Friday and the Prophet was standing at 'Arafat' (Sahih al-Bukhari 45).\n\n'But whoever is compelled by extreme hunger' — the law bends where life is at stake.",
  "4": "'They ask you, ˹O Prophet,˺ what is permissible for them ˹to eat˺. Say, \"What is good and lawful. Also what is caught by your hunting animals and birds of prey which you have trained as instructed by Allah. So eat what they catch for you, but mention the Name of Allah over it ˹first˺.\" And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is swift in reckoning.' (Quran 5:4). A question about hunting, answered with a principle.\n\n'What is good and lawful' — uhilla lakumu al-tayyibat. 'Tayyib' means wholesome, clean, pleasant. The category is broader than the question asked.\n\n'Hunting animals and birds of prey which you have trained' — al-jawarih mukallibin. Trained dogs and hawks. The Arabic word for trained comes from the same root as 'kalb', dog, and carries the sense of an animal taught to work for its handler.\n\n'Adi ibn Hatim said: I asked the Messenger of Allah, 'We hunt with these hounds.' He said: 'If you send your trained hounds after a game and mention Allah's Name on sending, you can eat of what they catch for you. But if the hound eats of the game, then you must not eat of it, for I am afraid that the hound caught it for itself, and if another hound joins your hounds during the hunt, you should not eat of the game' (Sahih al-Bukhari 5487).\n\nThat hadith explains 'what they catch for you' precisely: the test is whether the animal held the game for its owner or took it for itself.\n\n'Mention the Name of Allah over it' — the name is said at the moment of release, since the hunter is not present at the kill. Even at a distance, the act is opened by naming Him.",
  "5": "'Today all good, pure foods have been made lawful for you. Similarly, the food of the People of the Book is permissible for you and yours is permissible for them. And ˹permissible for you in marriage˺ are chaste believing women as well as chaste women of those given the Scripture before you—as long as you pay them their dowries in wedlock, neither fornicating nor taking them as mistresses. And whoever rejects the faith, all their good deeds will be void ˹in this life˺ and in the Hereafter they will be among the losers.' (Quran 5:5). Two permissions that open the door to ordinary life with neighbours.\n\n'The food of the People of the Book is permissible for you' — ta'amu alladhina utu al-kitaba hillun lakum. Their slaughtered meat may be eaten. And the sentence runs both ways: 'and yours is permissible for them'.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that the permission concerns the slaughter of Jews and Christians specifically, since they slaughter invoking the name of God.\n\nThen marriage. A Muslim man may marry a chaste Jewish or Christian woman, with three conditions attached in the same breath: pay the dowry, marry properly, and no arrangement outside marriage — 'neither fornicating nor taking them as mistresses'.\n\nNotice how carefully the verse guards the woman's position. The permission is not a loophole for a casual relationship; the dowry and the formal contract are named as part of the licence itself.\n\n'And whoever rejects the faith, all their good deeds will be void.' The warning closes the passage because closeness carries risk as well as benefit. Sharing meals and households with people of another faith is permitted; losing your own is not a small matter.\n\nThe picture is of a confident community, not a sealed one.",
  "6": "'O believers! When you rise up for prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe your heads, and wash your feet to the ankles... It is not Allah's Will to burden you, but to purify you and complete His favour upon you, so perhaps you will be grateful.' (Quran 5:6). The verse every Muslim's daily practice rests on.\n\nFour acts of wudu are named in order: face, hands to the elbows, wipe the head, feet to the ankles. Three are washing; one is wiping. The distinction is in the Arabic verbs and it is the basis of the whole fiqh of ablution.\n\nThen ghusl for major impurity, and then the concession: illness, travel, or no water available — 'purify yourselves with clean earth by wiping your faces and hands'. Tayammum reduces four limbs to two and water to dust.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: 'When a bondsman — a Muslim or a believer — washes his face, every sin he contemplated with his eyes will be washed away from his face along with the water... and when he washes his feet, every sin towards which his feet have walked will be washed away with the water, with the result that he comes out pure from all sins' (Sahih Muslim 244).\n\nSo the limbs washed are the limbs that sin. Wudu is a physical act with a moral shape.\n\n'It is not Allah's Will to burden you' — ma yuridu Allahu li yaj'ala 'alaykum min haraj. 'Haraj' is constriction, difficulty. The verse states the purpose of the law out loud, which the Quran rarely does.",
  "7": "'Remember Allah's favour upon you and the covenant He made with you when you said, \"We hear and obey.\" And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart.' (Quran 5:7). Back to the covenant of 5:1, now as something they said out loud.\n\n'We hear and obey' — sami'na wa ata'na. This is the believers' answer, and the Quran places it at the end of Surah al-Baqarah as their defining response: 'And they say, \"We hear and obey\"' (Quran 2:285).\n\nIt is worth setting beside its opposite. Of an earlier community the Quran reports: 'they answered, \"We hear and disobey\"' (Quran 2:93). The first half of the sentence is identical. Everyone hears. The second half is the whole difference.\n\n'Remember Allah's favour upon you' — the favour and the covenant are mentioned together, because the pledge was not extracted from people who had received nothing. Surah Ali 'Imran spells out what the favour was: 'when you were enemies, then He united your hearts, so you—by His grace—became brothers' (Quran 3:103).\n\n'Surely Allah knows best what is hidden in the heart' — bi dhati al-sudur. Literally: what the chests contain.\n\nThat ending is the point of the verse. A pledge spoken aloud can be sincere or hollow, and no observer can tell the difference. The One receiving the pledge can.\n\nSo the question this verse leaves you with is not whether you said it. It is whether you meant it, and whether you still do.",
  "8": "'O believers! Stand firm for Allah and bear true testimony. Do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice. Be just! That is closer to righteousness. And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is All-Aware of what you do.' (Quran 5:8). The companion of 4:135, aimed at the opposite failing.\n\nRead the two together. Surah an-Nisa said: 'Stand firm for justice as witnesses for Allah even if it is against yourselves, your parents, or close relatives' (Quran 4:135). That verse guards against bending justice toward the people you love. This one guards against bending it away from the people you hate.\n\nNotice the small reversal in the wording. There it was 'qawwamina bi al-qist' — standing firm for justice, as witnesses for Allah. Here it is 'qawwamina lillahi shuhada'a bi al-qist' — standing firm for Allah, as witnesses for justice. The two elements swap places, so that between them every arrangement is covered.\n\n'Do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice' — the same construction used in 5:2 about the Quraysh. The surah has now said it twice in six verses, which suggests how hard it is.\n\n'Be just! That is closer to righteousness' — i'dilu, huwa aqrabu lil-taqwa. Justice is named as the nearest thing to God-consciousness. Not prayer, not fasting — in this context, fairness to someone you dislike.\n\nThe practical test is simple and unpleasant: can you state your opponent's position accurately? Can you concede the point where he is right?",
  "9": "'Allah has promised those who believe and do good ˹His˺ forgiveness and a great reward.' (Quran 5:9). A promise placed immediately after a hard command.\n\nVerse 5:8 asked for justice toward enemies. This verse follows it, and the placement is the encouragement.\n\n'Allah has promised' — wa'ada Allah. In Arabic, 'wa'd' is a promise of good; a threat has a different word, 'wa'id'. The Quran is careful about which it uses.\n\nTwo things are promised, and the order repays attention. 'Forgiveness' — maghfirah — comes first. Then 'a great reward' — ajrun 'azim.\n\nWhy forgiveness first for people who 'believe and do good'? Because nobody meets the standard of 5:8 perfectly. Everyone who tries to be fair to someone he dislikes will fall short somewhere. The promise covers the shortfall before it mentions the prize.\n\nAl-Tabari, commenting on this verse, notes that the promise is bound to the two conditions stated — faith and righteous action — and that Allah does not break what He has promised.\n\nThe Quran often uses this pattern of a pairing: a demand, then a promise; a warning, then an opening. Verse 5:10 will supply the other side.\n\nThe point for a reader is that the standard being set in this surah is high, and it is not being set by someone who expects perfection. Effort is what is asked for, and the gap is already accounted for.",
  "10": "'As for those who disbelieve and deny Our signs, they are the residents of the Hellfire.' (Quran 5:10). The matching half of 5:9.\n\nThe Quran pairs these constantly — a promise, then its opposite. The purpose is balance: hope without complacency, fear without despair.\n\n'Those who disbelieve and deny Our signs' — kafaru wa kadhdhabu bi ayatina. Two verbs, not one. 'Kufr' is covering over, refusing. 'Takdhib' is calling something a lie — an active, public rejection.\n\nNote that 5:9 also used two elements: believe and do good. So each side of the pair has an inward part and an outward one. Faith and action; denial and the assertion of denial.\n\n'Our signs' — ayat. The word covers verses of scripture and signs in the created world alike, which is why the Quran uses one term for both.\n\n'Residents of the Hellfire' — ashabu al-jahim. 'Ashab' means companions, the people associated with a place, the ones who belong to it. It is the same construction used for 'the companions of the Garden'.\n\nThat word choice is deliberate. It presents the outcome as a matter of belonging rather than sentence — people end up in the company they spent their lives choosing.\n\nSet against 5:9, the two verses frame everything the surah is about to demand. The rules that follow are not arbitrary. They are the shape of one of these two paths.",
  "11": "'O believers! Remember Allah's favour upon you: when a people sought to harm you, but He held their hands back from you. Be mindful of Allah. And in Allah let the believers put their trust.' (Quran 5:11). A specific rescue, recalled.\n\n'When a people sought to harm you' — idh hamma qawmun an yabsutu ilaykum aydiyahum. Literally: when a people intended to stretch out their hands against you. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records several reported occasions, including a plot against the Prophet and his companions that was exposed before it could be carried out.\n\nThe Quran leaves the incident unnamed. That is characteristic: the lesson is portable precisely because the details are not fixed to one event.\n\n'He held their hands back from you' — fa kaffa aydiyahum 'ankum. The image is physical. Hands were already reaching; something stopped them.\n\nHere is what makes this verse worth sitting with. You are being asked to remember a danger you may never have seen. The plot that was abandoned, the accident that did not happen, the person who changed his mind — none of these appear in your memory as favours, because nothing occurred.\n\n'Remember Allah's favour' therefore includes an entire category of blessing that is invisible by nature. The harm that never arrived leaves no trace.\n\n'And in Allah let the believers put their trust' — wa 'ala Allahi fa al yatawakkali al-mu'minun. Tawakkul follows naturally from the thought. If He has already stopped hands you never saw, the ones you do see are not the real measure of your safety.",
  "12": "'Allah made a covenant with the Children of Israel and appointed twelve leaders from among them and ˹then˺ said, \"I am truly with you. If you establish prayer, pay alms-tax, believe in My messengers, support them, and lend to Allah a good loan, I will certainly forgive your sins and admit you into Gardens under which rivers flow. And whoever among you disbelieves afterwards has truly strayed from the Right Way.\"' (Quran 5:12). A covenant quoted with its terms attached.\n\n'Twelve leaders' — ithnay 'ashara naqiba. A 'naqib' is a chief responsible for a group, an overseer answerable for those under him. One for each tribe.\n\nThen five conditions: prayer, zakah, belief in the messengers, supporting them, and 'lending to Allah a good loan' — qardan hasana. The last phrase is remarkable. Spending in His cause is called a loan to the One who owns everything, which means it is described from the giver's side: something advanced and certain to be returned.\n\n'I am truly with you' — inni ma'akum. The promise of company comes before the conditions, not after. He is with them, and then tells them how to remain in that state.\n\nNote the exact parallel with the Muslim community. This surah opened by telling believers to honour their covenants, and the covenant described here contains prayer, charity and support for the Messenger — the same terms.\n\nThat is the reason the story is being told. Verses 5:12 to 5:14 are not a report about other people. They are a warning delivered by example, and 5:13 will show what happened next.",
  "13": "'But for breaking their covenant, We condemned them and hardened their hearts. They distorted the words of the Scripture and neglected a portion of what they had been commanded to uphold. You ˹O Prophet˺ will always find deceit on their part, except for a few. But pardon them and bear with them. Indeed, Allah loves the good-doers.' (Quran 5:13). What breaking a covenant does to a person.\n\n'We condemned them and hardened their hearts' — wa ja'alna qulubahum qasiyah. 'Qaswah' is hardness, the quality of stone. A hard heart still functions; it simply no longer responds.\n\nThe sequence is the lesson. The covenant was broken first; the hardness came after. Verse 4:155 made the same correction when they claimed their hearts were already sealed.\n\n'They distorted the words of the Scripture' — yuharrifuna al-kalima 'an mawadi'ih. Literally: they shift the words from their places. The Arabic describes moving something rather than destroying it, which is how tahrif usually works — a text kept, an emphasis relocated.\n\n'And neglected a portion of what they had been commanded to uphold' — the second failing, and the quieter one. Nothing was denied. Part of it was simply allowed to fall out of use.\n\nThen, unexpectedly: 'But pardon them and bear with them' — fa'fu 'anhum wasfah. After a full page of charges, the Prophet is told to overlook. 'Safh' means to turn the page, to present a smooth surface.\n\n'Indeed, Allah loves the good-doers' — the reason given for pardon is not that the offence was small. It is that ihsan is what He loves.",
  "14": "'And from those who say, \"We are Christians,\" We took their covenant, but they neglected a portion of what they had been commanded to uphold. So We let hostility and enmity arise between them until the Day of Judgment, and soon Allah will inform them of all they have done.' (Quran 5:14). The same charge, a different community.\n\n'Those who say, \"We are Christians\"' — alladhina qalu inna nasara. The phrasing reports their self-description rather than endorsing it, the same way 2:14 reports the hypocrites' claim.\n\n'They neglected a portion of what they had been commanded to uphold' — nasu hazzan mimma dhukkiru bih. The identical clause used of the previous community in 5:13. The Quran repeats it word for word, which is the point: this is one failure occurring twice.\n\n'Nasu' can mean forgot or abandoned. Both readings work, and together they describe how it actually happens — not a decision to drop a teaching, but a slow ceasing to mention it until nobody remembers it was there.\n\n'So We let hostility and enmity arise between them' — fa aghrayna baynahumu al-'adawata wa al-baghda'a. The consequence of neglecting revelation is internal division. Once the shared reference point is gone, there is nothing left to settle disputes, and each faction becomes the enemy of the others.\n\nThat is the warning the whole passage has been building toward, and Surah Ali 'Imran stated the antidote directly: 'hold firmly to the rope of Allah and do not be divided' (Quran 3:103).\n\nA community that lets part of its guidance quietly lapse should expect to start fighting itself.",
  "15": "'O People of the Book! Now Our Messenger has come to you, revealing much of what you have hidden of the Scriptures and disregarding much. There certainly has come to you from Allah a light and a clear Book' (Quran 5:15). An invitation that follows two verses of charges.\n\n'Revealing much of what you have hidden' — yubayyinu lakum kathiran mimma kuntum tukhfuna mina al-kitab. The verb is 'bayyana', to make clear. This connects directly to 5:13, where the charge was moving words from their places.\n\nThen the half of the sentence that is easy to miss: 'and disregarding much' — wa ya'fu 'an kathir. He passes over much of it. The Quran does not set out to expose everything that was concealed. It corrects what affects belief and practice, and leaves the rest.\n\nThat is a striking editorial restraint, and it says something about the purpose of the correction. The aim is guidance, not humiliation.\n\n'A light and a clear Book' — nurun wa kitabun mubin. Two things, or one thing described twice. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, reads the light as the Prophet himself and the clear Book as the Quran.\n\nBoth readings carry the same practical sense. Light is not information; it is what makes everything else visible.\n\nNote the tone shift in the passage. Verses 5:12 to 5:14 catalogued broken covenants. This verse turns and offers the remedy to the same audience, in the same breath. The Quran's criticism is almost always followed by an open door.",
  "16": "'through which Allah guides those who seek His pleasure to the ways of peace, brings them out of darkness and into light by His Will, and guides them to the Straight Path.' (Quran 5:16). What the light of 5:15 actually does.\n\nThree effects are named, and they describe a journey.\n\n'Guides those who seek His pleasure to the ways of peace' — subula al-salam. Note the plural: ways, not way. There are many routes into safety. 'Salam' is peace, soundness, wholeness — the root of the greeting Muslims exchange and one of Allah's own names.\n\n'Brings them out of darkness and into light' — mina al-zulumati ila al-nur. Look at the number again, and notice it reverses. Darkness is plural, light is singular. The Quran is consistent about this throughout: there are countless ways to be lost and one way to be found.\n\n'By His Will' — bi idhnih. Placed in the middle of the sentence to prevent a misunderstanding. The book is the means; the guidance is His act.\n\n'And guides them to the Straight Path' — sirat mustaqim. Singular again, and now definite. The plural 'ways of peace' narrows to one path.\n\nThat progression is the structure of the verse: many entrances, one road.\n\nBut everything rests on the condition stated at the start — 'those who seek His pleasure', mani ittaba'a ridwanah. The light is available to everyone; it guides the one who wants what it leads to.",
  "17": "'Indeed, those who say, \"Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,\" have fallen into disbelief. Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Who has the power to prevent Allah if He chose to destroy the Messiah, son of Mary, his mother, and everyone in the world all together?\" To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything in between. He creates whatever He wills. And Allah is Most Capable of everything.' (Quran 5:17). An argument from vulnerability.\n\nThe reasoning is direct. If the Messiah could be destroyed, he is not God — and nobody claims he could not have been. The very fact that the question can be asked settles it.\n\nNotice that the verse names 'his mother' alongside him. Maryam holds the highest rank of any woman in the Quran, and she is placed in the same sentence for the same reason: honour does not make a creature into a creator.\n\n'Son of Mary' — ibn Maryam. The Quran uses this title repeatedly, and the choice is an argument in itself. Anyone who has a mother has a beginning.\n\n'To Allah alone belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything in between' — 'everything in between' closes the last gap. There is no intermediate category, no half-divine tier between Creator and creation.\n\n'He creates whatever He wills' — yakhluqu ma yasha'. This answers the argument from the virgin birth. A birth without a father is unusual; it is not divine. Surah Ali 'Imran makes the comparison exact: 'the example of Jesus in the sight of Allah is like that of Adam' (Quran 3:59) — created without either parent.",
  "18": "'The Jews and the Christians each say, \"We are the children of Allah and His most beloved!\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Why then does He punish you for your sins? No! You are only humans like others of His Own making. He forgives whoever He wills and punishes whoever He wills...\"' (Quran 5:18). A claim answered with one question.\n\n'We are the children of Allah and His most beloved' — nahnu abna'u Allahi wa ahibba'uh. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, explains the claim as one of privileged standing rather than literal descent — the assertion of a relationship so close that judgment does not really apply to them.\n\nThe reply is a single question: 'Why then does He punish you for your sins?' — fa lima yu'adhdhibukum bi dhunubikum. You concede that punishment happens. A favourite is not punished. The claim collapses on evidence the claimants themselves accept.\n\n'No! You are only humans like others of His Own making' — bal antum basharun mimman khalaq. Basher: flesh and blood. Part of the same creation as everyone else.\n\nThis is the same argument Surah an-Nisa made in 4:123: 'It is not by your wishes or the wishes of the People of the Book!' The Quran addressed Muslims first in that verse, which is worth remembering here.\n\nBecause the temptation is universal. Every religious community eventually produces the belief that membership itself is the safeguard.\n\n'He forgives whoever He wills and punishes whoever He wills' — the decision stays with Him, and it is not delegated to a group's roster.",
  "19": "'O People of the Book! Our Messenger has indeed come to you, making things clear to you after an interval between the messengers so you do not say, \"There has never come to us a deliverer of good news or a warner.\" Now there has come to you a deliverer of good news and a warner. And Allah is Most Capable of everything.' (Quran 5:19). The gap, and why it had to be filled.\n\n'After an interval between the messengers' — 'ala fatratin mina al-rusul. 'Fatrah' means a pause, a slackening, a period when something lapses. Roughly six centuries separated 'Isa from Muhammad, peace be upon them both.\n\n'So you do not say, \"There has never come to us a deliverer of good news or a warner\"' — the reason is stated as removing an excuse. This is exactly the principle of 4:165: messengers were sent 'so humanity should have no excuse before Allah after the coming of the messengers'.\n\nThen the sentence answers itself: 'Now there has come to you a deliverer of good news and a warner.' The objection is voiced and closed in the same verse.\n\n'Bashir wa nadhir' — bringer of good news and warner. Both, always in that order in the Quran.\n\nThere is a quiet mercy in the structure of this passage. Verses 5:15 to 5:19 are addressed to the People of the Book four times, and each address begins with what has come to them rather than what they did wrong.\n\nThe charges in 5:13 and 5:14 were real. But the Quran spends more verses inviting than accusing, and the invitation always comes last.",
  "20": "'And ˹remember˺ when Moses said to his people, \"O my people! Remember Allah's favours upon you when He raised prophets from among you, made you sovereign, and gave you what He had never given anyone in the world.\"' (Quran 5:20). The start of a story told as a warning.\n\nThree favours are listed: prophets from among them, sovereignty, and something unique in their time.\n\n'Made you sovereign' — wa ja'alakum muluka. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that after generations of slavery under Pharaoh they became masters of their own affairs, with homes, families and property of their own — which for a people freshly out of bondage is itself a kind of kingship.\n\n'And gave you what He had never given anyone in the world' — the parting of the sea, the manna and quails, the cloud that shaded them. Surah al-Baqarah lists them: 'We shaded you with clouds and sent down to you manna and quails' (Quran 2:57).\n\nNotice what Musa is doing. He is not issuing a command yet; that comes in 5:21. He begins by recounting what they have already received.\n\nThat is the standard order in the Quran, and it appeared in 5:7 with this community: remember the favour, then honour the covenant. Gratitude is presented as the ground that obedience grows in.\n\nThe story that follows is included in Surah al-Ma'idah for one reason. This surah opened by demanding that covenants be kept, and here is a people about to break one, with the favours still fresh.",
  "21": "'O my people! Enter the Holy Land which Allah has destined for you ˹to enter˺. And do not turn back or else you will become losers.' (Quran 5:21). A command with the outcome already promised.\n\n'Which Allah has destined for you' — allati kataba Allahu lakum. Literally: which Allah has written for you. The land was assigned. The victory was not in doubt.\n\nThat is what makes the refusal in 5:22 so serious. They were not asked to gamble. They were told the result in advance and asked to walk in.\n\n'Do not turn back' — wa la tartaddu 'ala adbarikum. The Arabic is vivid: do not turn upon your backs. It is the language of a battlefield retreat.\n\n'Or else you will become losers' — fa tanqalibu khasirin. 'Inqilab' is turning over, being overturned. A people who reverse direction are not merely delayed; their situation inverts.\n\nHere is the tension worth noticing. Allah wrote the land for them, and they still had to enter it. The decree did not carry them in.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's steadiest teachings about destiny. A promised outcome does not remove the required effort; it guarantees that the effort will not be wasted.\n\nMany people misuse the idea in the opposite direction — treating a promise as a reason to wait. The generation in this story did exactly that, and 5:26 records the cost.",
  "22": "'They replied, \"O Moses! There is an enormously powerful people there, so we will never ˹be able to˺ enter it until they leave. If they do, then we will enter!\"' (Quran 5:22). The refusal, phrased as a condition.\n\nRead the logic carefully. They will enter the land — once the people occupying it have left. In other words, they will take possession when there is nothing left to take possession of.\n\n'There is an enormously powerful people there' — qawman jabbarin. 'Jabbar' means overbearing, one who compels by force. The description may well have been accurate. Their assessment of the enemy was not the problem.\n\nThe problem is what they left out. Verse 5:21 had just told them Allah had written the land for them. Their reply weighs the enemy and never mentions the promise.\n\n'We will never be able to enter it' — inna lan nadkhulaha. 'Lan' is absolute negation of the future: never.\n\nThis is a recognisable human move rather than an ancient curiosity. The conditions are set so high that nothing is technically refused, and nothing is ever done. It preserves the appearance of willingness.\n\nSurah al-Baqarah records the same pattern in a later generation: they demanded a king so they could fight, 'but when they were ordered to fight, they fled, except for a few of them' (Quran 2:246).\n\nThe willingness was sincere while it cost nothing.",
  "23": "'Two God-fearing men—who had been blessed by Allah—said, \"Surprise them through the gate. If you do, you will certainly prevail. Put your trust in Allah if you are ˹truly˺ believers.\"' (Quran 5:23). Two voices against a crowd.\n\n'Two God-fearing men' — rajulani mina alladhina yakhafun. Two, out of an entire people. The Quran preserves the minority position in almost every one of these accounts.\n\nTheir advice is strikingly practical: 'Surprise them through the gate' — udkhulu 'alayhimu al-bab. Not a sermon. A plan. Get through the entrance and the fortification stops working for the defenders.\n\nThen the assurance: 'If you do, you will certainly prevail' — fa idha dakhaltumuhu fa innakum ghalibun. Enter, and the outcome follows.\n\nAnd only then: 'Put your trust in Allah if you are truly believers' — wa 'ala Allahi fa tawakkalu.\n\nThe order is the lesson. Tawakkul is mentioned after the tactical advice, not instead of it. Trust in Allah in the Quran is never a substitute for taking the gate; it is what you do while taking it.\n\nSurah al-Baqarah records the same reasoning from another minority: 'How many times has a small force vanquished a mighty army by the Will of Allah!' (Quran 2:249).\n\n'Who had been blessed by Allah' — an'ama Allahu 'alayhima. The favour mentioned is not wealth or rank. In context it is the fear of Allah that let them see the situation correctly when everyone around them could not.",
  "24": "'˹Yet˺ they said, \"O Moses! ˹Still˺ we will never enter as long as they remain there. So go—both you and your Lord—and fight; we are staying right here!\"' (Quran 5:24). The refusal, now insolent.\n\n'So go—both you and your Lord—and fight' — fadhhab anta wa rabbuka fa qatila. The words are shocking, and the Quran preserves them exactly. This is not doubt about the plan; it is contempt aimed at Allah and His prophet together.\n\n'We are staying right here' — inna hahuna qa'idun. Literally: we are sitting here. The verb for sitting is used throughout the Quran for those who stay behind while others go out — it appeared in 4:95 contrasting 'those who sit at home' with those who strive.\n\nNotice the deterioration across three verses. In 5:22 they gave a reason and a condition. Here, after being shown the plan and offered the assurance, the excuse is dropped and only the refusal remains.\n\nThat is how it usually goes. An argument answered does not always produce agreement; sometimes it just removes the cover from what was underneath.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes the contrast the Prophet's companions later drew with it. At Badr, al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad told the Prophet that they would not say what the people of Musa said, but rather: go, and we fight with you.\n\nThe same test, offered to two communities, answered in opposite ways.",
  "25": "'Moses pleaded, \"My Lord! I have no control over anyone except myself and my brother. So set us apart from the rebellious people.\"' (Quran 5:25). A prophet at the end of his options.\n\n'I have no control over anyone except myself and my brother' — la amliku illa nafsi wa akhi. He is a prophet who has shown them the sea split open, and he cannot move them one step.\n\nThat admission is worth pausing on. Guidance cannot be imposed, not even by a messenger with miracles behind him. The Quran states the principle plainly: 'Let there be no compulsion in religion' (Quran 2:256).\n\n'My brother' — Harun. The one person who stood with him. Musa had asked for him at the start of his mission, and here that request is quietly vindicated.\n\n'So set us apart from the rebellious people' — fafruq baynana wa bayna al-qawmi al-fasiqin. 'Farq' is separation, division. He asks not for their destruction but for a distinction to be made.\n\n'Fasiq' is one who has broken out — the word describes a date leaving its skin. It names people who have stepped outside the boundary rather than people who never knew where it was.\n\nThere is honest exhaustion in this prayer, and the Quran does not tidy it away. Prophets are shown reaching the limit of what they can do with people, and taking that limit to Allah.\n\nWhich is, in the end, the only place it can be taken.",
  "26": "'Allah replied, \"Then this land is forbidden to them for forty years, during which they will wander through the land. So do not grieve for the rebellious people.\"' (Quran 5:26). The consequence, measured in a generation.\n\n'Forbidden to them for forty years' — muharramatun 'alayhim arba'ina sanah. The land they refused to enter becomes unlawful for them to enter.\n\nThe punishment is precisely the shape of the crime. They said they would not go in; they are told they may not.\n\n'During which they will wander through the land' — yatihuna fi al-ard. 'Tih' is aimless wandering, movement without arrival. They wanted to avoid a journey with a destination, and they received a journey without one.\n\nWhy forty years? Long enough for that generation to pass. The land was entered eventually — by their children, who had not been in the room when the refusal was made.\n\nThat is the sober part of this story. The promise of 5:21 was kept. It was simply kept for someone else.\n\n'So do not grieve for the rebellious people' — wa la ta'sa 'ala al-qawmi al-fasiqin. Musa is told to stop carrying it. He had done what he could, said what he was given to say, and the outcome was no longer his responsibility.\n\nFor anyone who has tried and failed to move people they care about, this last line is the practical one. There is a point at which grief over another's choice becomes a burden Allah has not assigned you.",
  "27": "'Relate to them in truth ˹O Prophet˺ the story of Adam's two sons—how each offered a sacrifice: Abel's offering was accepted while Cain's was not. So Cain threatened, \"I will kill you!\" His brother replied, \"Allah only accepts ˹the offering˺ of the sincerely devout.\"' (Quran 5:27). The first murder, and the reason for it.\n\n'Relate to them in truth' — watlu 'alayhim naba'a ibnay Adama bi al-haqq. The instruction to tell it truthfully suggests other versions were circulating.\n\nBoth brothers made an offering. One was accepted. That is the entire trigger.\n\nNotice what the rejected brother does with the news. He does not ask why his own offering failed. He moves directly to removing the person whose offering succeeded — which fixes nothing, and he must have known it.\n\nThat is the anatomy of envy the Quran is exposing. Envy does not want to rise; it wants the other to fall.\n\n'Allah only accepts the offering of the sincerely devout' — innama yataqabbalu Allahu mina al-muttaqin. The answer is not a taunt. It is the diagnosis, and it names the one variable that was in his brother's control.\n\nThe lesson is uncomfortable and useful. If your work was not accepted, the question is about your taqwa, not about the person whose work was.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: 'Whenever a person is murdered unjustly, there is a share from the burden of the crime on the first son of Adam, for he was the first to start the tradition of murdering' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3335).",
  "28": "'If you raise your hand to kill me, I will not raise mine to kill you, because I fear Allah—the Lord of all worlds.' (Quran 5:28). The reply of the man who was about to die.\n\n'I will not raise mine to kill you' — ma ana bi basitin yadiya ilayka li aqtulak. He states it as a settled fact about himself, not as a plea.\n\nAnd note the reason he gives: 'because I fear Allah'. Not because he is weak, not because he loves his brother, not because he doubts he would win the fight. The restraint has one source.\n\nThis raises a real question, since Islam permits self-defence. Commentators including Ibn Kathir explain that he refused to be the one who initiates killing — he would not become an aggressor even under threat.\n\nAbu Bakrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'When two Muslims confront one another with swords, both the slayer and the slain would be in Fire.' It was asked: this is the slayer, but what about the slain? He said: 'He also intended to kill his companion' (Sahih Muslim 2888a).\n\nThat hadith explains the principle exactly. What condemns a man is the intention to kill, and it condemns him whether or not he succeeds.\n\n'The Lord of all worlds' — rabb al-'alamin. He invokes the title that covers his brother too. The One he fears is also his brother's Lord, which is why the threat does not change what he owes.",
  "29": "'I want to let you bear your sin against me along with your other sins, then you will be one of those destined to the Fire. And that is the reward of the wrongdoers.' (Quran 5:29). The second half of the innocent brother's reply.\n\nThis line troubles readers, because it sounds like he wants his brother in the Fire. That is not what it says.\n\n'I want to let you bear' — inni uridu an tabu'a bi ithmi wa ithmik. Commentators including al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir explain the meaning as: if one of us must carry the sin of this encounter, I intend that it be you and not me. He is stating his refusal to become the aggressor, expressed in terms of who ends up accountable.\n\nIn other words, the sentence is about his own choice, not a wish about his brother's fate. He would rather be killed carrying nothing than survive carrying a murder.\n\n'Your sin against me along with your other sins' — ithmi wa ithmik. Literally: my sin and your sin. The natural reading is the sin of killing me, added to what you already carry.\n\n'That is the reward of the wrongdoers' — wa dhalika jaza'u al-zalimin. He names the outcome plainly, which is a final warning offered before the blow lands.\n\nWhat this passage models is a person who has done the moral arithmetic in advance. He is not improvising under threat; he already knows what he will and will not do.\n\nMost people discover their limits during the crisis. He had settled his beforehand.",
  "30": "'Yet Cain convinced himself to kill his brother, so he killed him—becoming a loser.' (Quran 5:30). The murder, described in five words of Arabic.\n\n'Convinced himself' — fa tawwa'at lahu nafsuhu qatla akhih. The verb 'tawwa'a' means to make something agreeable, to render it easy to accept. His own soul talked him into it.\n\nThat is the mechanism the verse is exposing. Nobody arrives at a great wrong in one step. The self negotiates first, softens the thing, finds the angle from which it looks permissible.\n\nYusuf's account names the same faculty: 'indeed the soul is ever inclined to evil, except those shown mercy by my Lord' (Quran 12:53).\n\nNotice how little space the Quran gives the act itself. Three verses of warning and dialogue precede it; the killing gets a clause. The Quran's interest is in the process, not the event.\n\n'So he killed him' — fa qatalah. Flat, without adjective or drama.\n\n'Becoming a loser' — fa asbaha mina al-khasirin. He got what he wanted. His rival was gone, the competition over, the offering no longer a rebuke to him. And the verse calls him a loser.\n\nThat is the verdict on every gain obtained this way. The thing was won; the person was lost.\n\n'Asbaha' literally means he became, or he woke up as. The morning after, he was someone else.",
  "31": "'Then Allah sent a crow digging ˹a grave˺ in the ground ˹for a dead crow˺, in order to show him how to bury the corpse of his brother. He cried, \"Alas! Have I ˹even˺ failed to be like this crow and bury the corpse of my brother?\" So he became regretful.' (Quran 5:31). The first man to kill, taught by a bird.\n\nThe image is deliberately humbling. The first human being to commit murder does not know what to do with the body. A crow shows him.\n\n'In order to show him' — li yuriyahu kayfa yuwari saw'ata akhih. 'Saw'ah' means that which is shameful to expose. The word is used in the Quran for what Adam and his wife rushed to cover. Here it is his brother's corpse, and the choice of word suggests the dead body is something whose exposure shames the living.\n\n'Have I even failed to be like this crow?' — a 'ajaztu an akuna mithla hadha al-ghurab. The comparison he draws is with an animal, and he finds himself below it.\n\n'So he became regretful' — fa asbaha mina al-nadimin. The same verb as 5:30: he became. First he became a loser, then he became regretful.\n\nBut note what he regrets. Reading the verse closely, the lament is about not knowing how to hide the body — not about the killing. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, discusses whether his remorse was repentance at all, since the Quran does not report him turning to Allah.\n\nRegret and repentance are not the same thing. One is a feeling about consequences; the other is a return.",
  "32": "'That is why We ordained for the Children of Israel that whoever takes a life—unless as a punishment for murder or mischief in the land—it will be as if they killed all of humanity; and whoever saves a life, it will be as if they saved all of humanity...' (Quran 5:32). The law drawn from the story just told.\n\n'That is why' — min ajli dhalik. The ruling is presented as the direct consequence of the first murder. One killing established a precedent, and the law responds to precedents.\n\n'As if they killed all of humanity' — fa ka annama qatala al-nasa jami'a. The equation is startling and deliberate. One life carries the weight of all lives, because the principle violated is the same one that protects everyone.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Whenever a person is murdered unjustly, there is a share from the burden of the crime on the first son of Adam, for he was the first to start the tradition of murdering' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3335). The hadith and the verse work the same way: acts of killing are connected to one another.\n\n'And whoever saves a life, it will be as if they saved all of humanity' — the positive side, and it is stated with exactly equal weight. Saving one person counts as saving everyone.\n\nThe exceptions are named — lawful punishment for murder, and fasad in the land — because the verse is a law, not a slogan.\n\n'Ordained for the Children of Israel' — the ruling is quoted from an earlier scripture, and the Quran endorses it. Some moral truths do not change between revelations.",
  "33": "'Indeed, the penalty for those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and spread mischief in the land is death, crucifixion, cutting off their hands and feet on opposite sides, or exile from the land. This ˹penalty˺ is a disgrace for them in this world, and they will suffer a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter.' (Quran 5:33). The hirabah ruling.\n\n'Those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger' — yuharibuna Allaha wa rasulah. The crime is 'hirabah': armed violence against the public, banditry, terrorising travellers and communities. It is not private crime and it is not dissent.\n\nAnas reported the incident behind the ruling: some men from the tribe of 'Ukl came to the Prophet and embraced Islam, but the climate of Madinah did not suit them, so he sent them to the charity camels to drink their milk. Once recovered, they killed the herdsman and drove the camels away. They were pursued and caught, and the penalty was carried out (Sahih al-Bukhari 6802).\n\nSo the offence is precisely defined: they murdered the man who had been caring for them and stole from the public charity.\n\nFour penalties are listed. Commentators including al-Tabari and al-Qurtubi differ over whether the judge chooses among them or whether each matches a particular degree of the crime — whether the offender killed, or stole, or both, or merely spread terror.\n\nThat debate matters. The graded reading ties each penalty to what was actually done, which is how most jurists applied it.\n\nRead on. The very next verse limits everything this one just said.",
  "34": "'As for those who repent before you seize them, then know that Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 5:34). The exception attached to the harshest penalty in the surah.\n\n'Before you seize them' — min qabli an taqdiru 'alayhim. Before you gain power over them. The window closes at capture.\n\nThat timing is the whole point. Repentance after arrest is worth nothing legally, because anyone would repent once caught. What is being rewarded is a man who stops while he still has the option to continue.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that this waives the hadd penalty of hirabah specifically, while the rights owed to victims — blood and stolen property — remain claimable.\n\nThat distinction is important and often missed. Allah waives His own claim. He does not waive what is owed to the people who were harmed.\n\nPut 5:33 and 5:34 side by side and the shape of Islamic criminal law becomes visible. The penalty is severe, and the door out of it is left standing open until the last possible moment.\n\n'All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' — Ghafurun Rahim. These are the two names that close the verse about armed murderers and highway robbers.\n\nWhich is worth remembering when the previous verse is quoted on its own. The Quran itself refused to let 5:33 stand as the final word.",
  "35": "'O believers! Be mindful of Allah and seek what brings you closer to Him and struggle in His Way, so you may be successful.' (Quran 5:35). Three instructions after a passage on crime and punishment.\n\n'Seek what brings you closer to Him' — wabtaghu ilayhi al-wasilah. 'Wasilah' is a means of approach, a way of drawing near.\n\nWhat is the wasilah? Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, cites Ibn 'Abbas, Mujahid and others explaining it as nearness sought through obedience and righteous deeds. The means of approach is what you do.\n\nThis matters, because the word has sometimes been used to argue for approaching Allah through intermediaries. The Quran describes the righteous themselves as seekers: 'Even the closest to Allah of those invoked would be seeking a way to their Lord' (Quran 17:57). The most honoured servants are looking for the same wasilah everyone else is.\n\n'And struggle in His Way' — wa jahidu fi sabilih. 'Jihad' is exertion, effort against resistance. The resistance may be an army, or it may be your own inclination — the same nafs that talked Cain into it in 5:30.\n\nNotice the sequence: taqwa, then seeking nearness, then striving. Awareness, direction, effort. Each one implies the next.\n\n'So you may be successful' — la'allakum tuflihun. 'Falah' is the word called out in the adhan five times a day. Its root means to split open the earth — the farmer's word, for something that breaks through after being buried.",
  "36": "'As for the disbelievers, even if they were to possess everything in the world twice over ˹and offer it all˺ to ransom themselves from the punishment of the Day of Judgment, it would never be accepted from them. And they will suffer a painful punishment.' (Quran 5:36). What money cannot do.\n\n'Everything in the world twice over' — ma fi al-ardi jami'an wa mithlahu ma'ah. All of it, and the same again. The Quran deliberately names an impossible sum to close off the calculation entirely.\n\nSurah Ali 'Imran makes the same point with gold: 'if each of those who disbelieve then die as disbelievers were to offer a ransom of enough gold to fill the whole world, it would never be accepted from them' (Quran 3:91).\n\nWhy is the point worth making twice? Because ransom is how the world actually works. Fines, settlements, payments to make a problem go away — most difficulties can be reduced to a price. The Quran is describing the one situation where that method fails.\n\n'It would never be accepted' — ma tuqubbila minhum. And note the reason it fails. It is not that the sum is too small. It is that the currency is wrong. What is required on that Day is what 5:35 described: taqwa, nearness, striving. None of it can be bought late.\n\nThere is something almost merciful in stating this so bluntly. A person who understands that the exit cannot be purchased at the end has every reason to attend to it now, while the means of approach are still available.",
  "37": "'They will be desperate to get out of the Fire but they will never be able to. And they will suffer an everlasting punishment.' (Quran 5:37). The attempt after the ransom fails.\n\n'They will be desperate to get out' — yuriduna an yakhruju mina al-nar. The verb is 'want', 'intend'. Not a plea — a sustained will to escape.\n\n'But they will never be able to' — wa ma hum bi kharijina minha. The Arabic construction negates it as a state, not just an event: they are not people who exit.\n\nSet this beside 5:36 and a sequence appears. First they try to pay their way out; the currency is refused. Then they try to force their way out; the exit does not exist.\n\nEvery human method is being closed in turn, which is the purpose of putting these two verses together.\n\n'An everlasting punishment' — 'adhabun muqim. 'Muqim' means resident, settled, staying in place. The same root as 'iqamah', taking up residence. The punishment is not passing through; it lives there.\n\nThe Quran presents this material without embellishment, and the restraint is the point. There is no lingering description, no dwelling on detail.\n\nThen 5:38 turns immediately to a matter of property law. That abruptness is characteristic of this surah: a scene of the Hereafter, then a rule about theft, because the two belong to one continuous reality.",
  "38": "'As for male and female thieves, cut off their hands for what they have done—a deterrent from Allah. And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 5:38). The hadd for theft.\n\n'Male and female thieves' — al-sariqu wa al-sariqah. Both named explicitly, and the male is mentioned first here, whereas in 24:2 for zina the female is named first. The Quran distributes the emphasis rather than assuming one sex is the default offender.\n\n'A deterrent from Allah' — nakalan mina Allah. 'Nakal' is an exemplary punishment, one whose purpose is to stop others.\n\nThe conditions that surround this penalty in Islamic law are extensive, and they are what make it workable: a minimum value of stolen goods, secure custody, no doubt in the evidence, and no theft driven by hunger. 'Umar suspended the penalty during a year of famine, and the jurists preserved that as a precedent.\n\nWhat the Quran emphasises here is not severity but impartiality. 'Aisha reported that when a woman of Makhzum committed theft, the Quraysh sought someone to intercede, and only Usamah dared to speak to the Prophet. He said: 'Do you intercede with me to violate one of the legal punishments of Allah?' Then he addressed the people: 'O people! The nations before you went astray because if a noble person committed theft, they used to leave him, but if a weak person among them committed theft, they used to inflict the legal punishment on him. By Allah, if Fatimah the daughter of Muhammad committed theft, Muhammad will cut off her hand' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6788).\n\nThe failure that destroys a legal system is named there, and it is not harshness. It is selective application.",
  "39": "'But whoever repents after their wrongdoing and mends their ways, Allah will surely turn to them in forgiveness. Indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 5:39). The same pattern as 5:33 and 5:34, repeated deliberately.\n\nA penalty, then a door. The surah has now done this twice in six verses, which tells you it is a structural principle rather than an afterthought.\n\n'Repents... and mends their ways' — taba min ba'di zulmihi wa aslah. Two verbs again, as in 4:146. 'Tawbah' is the inward turn; 'islah' is repair — putting right what was damaged, which in the case of theft plainly includes returning what was taken.\n\n'Allah will surely turn to them in forgiveness' — fa inna Allaha yatubu 'alayh. Notice the Arabic uses the same verb for both parties. The servant turns, and Allah turns to him. Repentance in the Quran is described as a mutual motion.\n\nThere is a legal point that keeps these verses honest. Repentance here concerns the standing of the sin before Allah; commentators including al-Qurtubi discuss separately whether it lifts the worldly penalty once the case has reached the authorities, and the majority held that it does not.\n\nSo the verse is not a loophole. A thief who repents is forgiven by Allah and still returns the property.\n\nWhat the placement teaches is how to read every hadd verse in the Quran. None of them is left standing alone. The mercy is not a softening added later by commentators; it is in the text, one verse away.",
  "40": "'Do you not know that the kingdom of the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah ˹alone˺? He punishes whoever He wills and forgives whoever He wills. And Allah is Most Capable of everything.' (Quran 5:40). The authority behind both the penalty and the pardon.\n\n'Do you not know' — a lam ta'lam. A rhetorical question, and it is addressed to the reader who has just been through 5:33 to 5:39.\n\nThose verses set out punishments and then opened doors. A person might reasonably ask which is the real position. This verse answers: both, and the choice belongs to the Owner.\n\n'He punishes whoever He wills and forgives whoever He wills' — the order is worth noting. In 5:18, addressing those who claimed to be Allah's beloved, the order was reversed: 'He forgives whoever He wills and punishes whoever He wills.'\n\nThe Quran adjusts the order to the audience. To people presuming on their status, mercy is named first and then the warning. To people who have just read a list of penalties and might despair, the punishment is named first and mercy has the last word.\n\n'The kingdom of the heavens and the earth' — mulku al-samawati wa al-ard. The word is ownership, not merely rule.\n\nThat is the ground of the whole passage. The laws in this surah are not a community's preferences. They belong to the One who owns the parties, the property and the outcome.",
  "41": "'O Messenger! Do not grieve for those who race to disbelieve—those who say, \"We believe\" with their tongues, but their hearts are in disbelief... They distort the Scripture, taking rulings out of context, then say, \"If this is the ruling you get from Muhammad, accept it. If not, beware!\"' (Quran 5:41). Judge-shopping, described exactly.\n\n'Do not grieve' — la yahzunka. The Prophet is being consoled before he is informed, which is how the Quran usually handles bad news for him.\n\n'Those who race to disbelieve' — yusari'una fi al-kufr. Racing: eager, competitive, hurrying toward it.\n\nTwo groups are named. Hypocrites who profess with the tongue, and 'those among the Jews who eagerly listen to lies' — sammaʿuna lil-kadhib, which can also mean listening in order to report back, acting as informants for others who would not come themselves.\n\nThen the strategy is quoted in their own words: 'If this is the ruling you get from Muhammad, accept it. If not, beware!' They approach the Prophet's court hoping for a particular verdict, having decided in advance to reject any other.\n\nThat is not seeking justice. It is shopping for a ruling, and the willingness to accept it is entirely conditional on the outcome.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records the case behind it: a ruling on adultery that they hoped would come out lighter than what their own scripture prescribed.\n\nThe behaviour has not aged. A person who consults scholars until one agrees with him is doing exactly this, and the verse names it as a disease of the heart rather than a question of law.",
  "42": "'They eagerly listen to falsehood and consume forbidden gain. So if they come to you O Prophet, either judge between them or turn away from them. If you turn away from them, they cannot harm you whatsoever. But if you judge between them, then do so with justice. Surely Allah loves those who are just.' (Quran 5:42). What to do with people who come to you in bad faith.\n\n'Consume forbidden gain' — akkaluna lil-suht. 'Suht' means what is unlawful and corrupting; commentators including Ibn Kathir explain it here as including bribery, which fits the judge-shopping described in 5:41 exactly. Someone was being paid.\n\nThen the Prophet is given a choice: 'either judge between them or turn away from them.' He is not obliged to hear a case brought by people who have already decided which verdict they will accept.\n\n'If you turn away from them, they cannot harm you whatsoever' — the reassurance addresses the real pressure. Declining to rule can feel like a loss of authority, and the verse says it is not.\n\n'But if you judge between them, then do so with justice' — the second condition is absolute. If you take the case, the bad faith of the parties changes nothing about your obligation. They came dishonestly; you rule honestly.\n\nThis is the same principle as 5:8: hostility from others never licenses unfairness from you.\n\n'Surely Allah loves those who are just' — inna Allaha yuhibbu al-muqsitin. The surah keeps returning to this. Justice is not merely commanded here; it is named as something He loves.",
  "43": "'But why do they come to you for judgment when they already have the Torah containing Allah's judgment, then they turn away after all? They are not true believers.' (Quran 5:43). The question that exposes the whole manoeuvre.\n\nThey have a scripture. It contains a ruling on the case. And they bring the case to someone whose authority they reject.\n\n'Abdullah ibn 'Umar reported that a Jewish man and woman who had committed adultery were brought to the Messenger of Allah. He asked the Jews: 'What do you find in the Torah for one who commits adultery?' They said they blacken their faces and parade them. He said: 'Bring the Torah if you are truthful.' They brought it and recited, and when the reader reached the verse of stoning he placed his hand over it and read around it. 'Abdullah ibn Salam, who was with the Prophet, said: command him to lift his hand. He lifted it, and underneath was the verse of stoning. The Prophet gave judgment and the penalty was carried out (Sahih Muslim 1699a).\n\nThat is this verse in action. The ruling was in their book, under a hand.\n\n'Then they turn away after all' — thumma yatawallawna min ba'di dhalik. They came for a verdict and left without it, because the verdict was not the one they wanted.\n\n'They are not true believers' — wa ma ula'ika bi al-mu'minin. Not believers in the Prophet they consulted, and — the sharper point — not believers in the Torah they claimed to follow either.\n\nA person who covers a verse with his hand does not believe the book he is holding.",
  "44": "'Indeed, We revealed the Torah, containing guidance and light, by which the prophets, who submitted themselves to Allah, made judgments for Jews. So too did the rabbis and scholars judge according to Allah's Book, with which they were entrusted and of which they were made keepers. So do not fear the people; fear Me! Nor trade my revelations for a fleeting gain. And those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are truly the disbelievers.' (Quran 5:44). The Torah defended, and its keepers held to account.\n\n'Guidance and light' — hudan wa nur. The same pair used of the Quran in 5:15. The Quran consistently honours the revelation it is correcting.\n\n'The prophets, who submitted themselves to Allah' — alladhina aslamu. They were muslims in the root sense of the word: people who submitted. The Quran applies the term to every prophet.\n\nThen the failure is located precisely. 'With which they were entrusted' — bima ustuhfizu min kitabi Allah. They were made custodians of a text, and custody is a trust — the same word the surah has been circling since 5:1.\n\nTwo motives for betraying it are named. 'Do not fear the people' — fear of what a community will say if you give the unpopular ruling. 'Nor trade my revelations for a fleeting gain' — thamanan qalila, a small price, which connects to the bribery of 5:42.\n\nFear and money. Between them they account for most corrupted verdicts in any age.\n\n'Those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are truly the disbelievers' — this sentence closes 5:44, 5:45 and 5:47, each time with a different word: disbelievers, wrongdoers, rebellious. Commentators including Ibn 'Abbas, cited by al-Tabari, read the severity as graded by whether the judge rejects the ruling outright or merely fails to apply it.",
  "45": "'We ordained for them in the Torah, \"A life for a life, an eye for an eye, a nose for a nose, an ear for an ear, a tooth for a tooth—and for wounds equal retaliation.\" But whoever waives it charitably, it will be atonement for them. And those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are truly the wrongdoers.' (Quran 5:45). Qisas, and the door out of it.\n\n'A life for a life' — al-nafsa bi al-nafs. The principle is exact equivalence. Not more than was taken, and not less.\n\nThis needs to be read as the reform it was. In a society of blood feuds, where one death could trigger a war between clans, a law that caps retaliation at precisely what was suffered is a restraint on violence, not a licence for it.\n\n'And for wounds equal retaliation' — wa al-juruha qisas. The same ceiling applies to injuries.\n\n'But whoever waives it charitably, it will be atonement for them' — fa man tasaddaqa bihi fa huwa kaffaratun lah. 'Tasaddaqa' is the verb of giving sadaqah. Forgiving your assailant is classified as an act of charity, and the Quran attaches a reward to it: it wipes away sins of your own.\n\nSo the law establishes the maximum and then makes the alternative more attractive. Surah al-Baqarah does the same with murder: 'if the offender is pardoned by the victim's guardian... This is a concession and a mercy from your Lord' (Quran 2:178).\n\nNote the shift in the closing formula. Verse 5:44 said 'disbelievers'; this says 'wrongdoers' — zalimun. A judge who denies a victim the qisas due to him has not merely erred. He has wronged a person.",
  "46": "'Then in the footsteps of the prophets, We sent Jesus, son of Mary, confirming the Torah revealed before him. And We gave him the Gospel containing guidance and light and confirming what was revealed in the Torah—a guide and a lesson to the God-fearing.' (Quran 5:46). The chain continues.\n\n'In the footsteps of the prophets' — 'ala atharihim. Following in their tracks. The Quran presents revelation as one road with many travellers, not a series of independent starts.\n\n'Confirming the Torah revealed before him' — musaddiqan lima bayna yadayhi mina al-tawrah. This is said twice in one verse, about 'Isa himself and about the Gospel he was given. The repetition is doing work: 'Isa did not arrive to abolish what came before.\n\n'The Gospel containing guidance and light' — fihi hudan wa nur. The identical phrase used of the Torah in 5:44 and of the Quran's arrival in 5:15. Three revelations, one description.\n\nThat consistency is the argument of this whole passage. A Muslim is required to believe that Allah sent the Torah and the Gospel, and to honour them as light — while holding that the Quran is the final and preserved authority, which 5:48 will state.\n\n'A guide and a lesson to the God-fearing' — hudan wa maw'izatan lil-muttaqin. 'Maw'izah' is admonition, the kind of counsel that moves a person to act.\n\nAnd notice who benefits: the God-fearing. The same book is available to everyone; it becomes guidance for those already inclined to receive it. Surah al-Baqarah opens with exactly this qualification in 2:2.",
  "47": "'So let the people of the Gospel judge by what Allah has revealed in it. And those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are truly the rebellious.' (Quran 5:47). The third of the three closing formulas.\n\n'Let the people of the Gospel judge by what Allah has revealed in it' — wal yahkum ahlu al-injili bima anzala Allahu fih. They are told to apply their own book.\n\nThat instruction reveals something about how this passage works. The Quran is not, in these verses, demanding that other communities adopt Islamic law. It is confronting them with the law they already claim to hold and asking why it is not being applied.\n\nWhich is the exact charge of 5:43: they had the ruling and covered it with a hand.\n\n'The rebellious' — al-fasiqun. Now complete the set. Verse 5:44: kafirun, disbelievers. Verse 5:45: zalimun, wrongdoers. Verse 5:47: fasiqun, rebellious.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on these verses, records the view that the three names describe three conditions of the same offence — rejecting the ruling as false, applying it unjustly, or abandoning it out of desire — with the first being disbelief and the others degrees below it.\n\nThe practical value of that distinction is considerable. It prevents the verse from being used to declare anyone who falls short in applying a ruling to be outside Islam, which is precisely how it has sometimes been misused.\n\nThree words, three failures, and the Quran was careful to use all three.",
  "48": "'We have revealed to you O Prophet this Book with the truth, as a confirmation of previous Scriptures and a supreme authority on them... To each of you We have ordained a code of law and a way of life. If Allah had willed, He would have made you one community, but His Will is to test you with what He has given each of you. So compete with one another in doing good...' (Quran 5:48). The summary of the whole passage, and one of the great verses of the Quran.\n\n'A supreme authority on them' — muhayminan 'alayh. 'Muhaymin' means guardian, overseer, the one who watches over and safeguards. The Quran confirms the earlier scriptures and stands above them as the criterion of what they now say.\n\n'To each of you We have ordained a code of law and a way of life' — shir'atan wa minhaja. 'Shir'ah' is a path to water; 'minhaj' is an open road. The laws given to different communities have differed. The core has not.\n\n'If Allah had willed, He would have made you one community' — the diversity is stated as deliberate. The Quran repeats it: 'Had your Lord so willed, He would have certainly made humanity one single community' (Quran 11:118).\n\n'But His Will is to test you with what He has given each of you' — the differences are the examination, not an accident to be resolved.\n\n'So compete with one another in doing good' — fastabiqu al-khayrat. Here is the response the Quran prescribes to religious difference. Not indifference, and not endless argument. Competition — in good.\n\nThat is a striking instruction. Given communities that will not agree, the Quran tells them to race each other in benefit, and leaves the verdict where it belongs: 'To Allah you will all return, then He will inform you of the truth regarding your differences.'",
  "49": "'And judge between them O Prophet by what Allah has revealed, and do not follow their desires. And beware, so they do not lure you away from some of what Allah has revealed to you...' (Quran 5:49). A warning about how compromise actually begins.\n\n'Do not follow their desires' — wa la tattabi' ahwa'ahum. 'Hawa' is inclination, what a person wants. The word has run through this passage since 5:48.\n\nThen the specific mechanism: 'so they do not lure you away from some of what Allah has revealed to you' — an yaftinuka 'an ba'di ma anzala Allahu ilayk.\n\nRead that word: 'some'. Ba'd. Not all of it. Nobody proposed that the Prophet abandon his religion. The pressure was to set aside one ruling, in one case, for one group.\n\nThat is how it is always done. The whole is never targeted, because the whole would be refused. A part is targeted, and the part is presented as reasonable, local and temporary.\n\nVerse 4:150 described the same technique from the other side: 'We believe in some and disbelieve in others.'\n\n'If they turn away from Allah's judgment, then know that it is Allah's Will to repay them for some of their sins' — again 'some'. Even the punishment mentioned is partial, which is its own kind of restraint.\n\nFor anyone in a position of responsibility, the practical warning is exact. Watch the single exception being requested. It is rarely presented as a change of principle, and it almost always is one.",
  "50": "'Is it the judgment of pre-Islamic ignorance they seek? Who could be a better judge than Allah for people of sure faith?' (Quran 5:50). Two questions that close the argument.\n\n'The judgment of pre-Islamic ignorance' — hukma al-jahiliyyah. 'Jahiliyyah' names the era before Islam, and its root is jahl — ignorance. But the Arabic sense is closer to recklessness than to lack of information: acting on impulse, pride and tribal loyalty rather than knowledge.\n\nThat is the definition worth keeping. Jahiliyyah is not a historical period that ended. It is a way of deciding things, and it is available in any century.\n\nThe verse frames the choice as binary. Either judgment comes from revelation, or it comes from somewhere else — and everything else, however sophisticated it looks, falls into the second category.\n\n'Who could be a better judge than Allah?' — wa man ahsanu mina Allahi hukman. The question answers itself, and it does so on grounds the surah has already laid out. He owns the parties (5:40), He knows what hearts contain (5:7), and He has no interest to serve.\n\nEvery human judge fails on at least one of those.\n\n'For people of sure faith' — li qawmin yuqinun. The final phrase places a condition on the argument. It is conclusive for those with yaqin — certainty. To someone without it, the question is genuinely open, which is why the Quran addresses belief before it addresses law.",
  "51": "'O believers! Take neither Jews nor Christians as guardians—they are guardians of each other. Whoever does so will be counted as one of them. Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.' (Quran 5:51). A verse frequently quoted without its context.\n\n'Guardians' — awliya'. This is the key word, and the translation matters enormously. A 'wali' is a protector, patron, the party whose side you are on in a conflict. It is a term of alliance, not of friendship.\n\nThe Quran uses a different vocabulary for ordinary good relations, and it is explicit about them: 'Allah does not forbid you from dealing kindly and fairly with those who have neither fought nor driven you out of your homes. Surely Allah loves those who are fair' (Quran 60:8). The next verse restricts the prohibition to 'those who have fought you for your faith, driven you out of your homes, or supported others in doing so' (Quran 60:9).\n\nThose two verses are the interpretive key. The prohibition concerns wartime allegiance, not neighbours, colleagues, business partners or in-laws.\n\nAnd this surah itself permits eating their food and marrying their women (5:5), and says of some of them: 'You will surely find... the most gracious to be those who call themselves Christian' (5:82). A blanket hostility cannot be squared with the same chapter.\n\n'They are guardians of each other' — the observation is about where their loyalty naturally sits, which is a statement of political reality.\n\nThe context is Madinah under threat, and 5:52 names the people the warning is actually about.",
  "52": "'You see those with sickness in their hearts racing for their guardianship, saying in justification, \"We fear a turn of fortune will strike us.\" But perhaps Allah will bring about your victory or another favour by His command, and they will regret what they have hidden in their hearts.' (Quran 5:52). Who 5:51 was aimed at.\n\n'Those with sickness in their hearts' — alladhina fi qulubihim marad. The Quran's standard description of the hypocrites of Madinah. This verse identifies the party rushing into these alliances, and they are not ordinary believers.\n\n'Racing for their guardianship' — yusari'una fihim. The same verb used in 5:41 of those who 'race to disbelieve'. Haste is repeatedly presented as a symptom.\n\n'We fear a turn of fortune will strike us' — nakhsha an tusibana da'irah. A 'da'irah' is a turn of the wheel, a reversal. This is the calculation stated aloud: the Muslims may lose, and we want a friend on the other side when they do.\n\nSo the sin is not contact with other communities. It is insurance against your own.\n\nCompare 4:139, where hypocrites sought 'izzah — honour — from the disbelievers. Same instinct, same verdict.\n\n'And they will regret what they have hidden in their hearts' — the secret is that they never believed the promise. Everything else follows from that.\n\nRead 5:51 without 5:52 and you get a rule about religions. Read them together and you get a rule about hedging, which is what the passage is actually describing.",
  "53": "'Only then, the believers will ask one another, \"Are these the ones who swore solemn oaths by Allah that they were with you?\" Their deeds have been in vain, so they have become losers.' (Quran 5:53). The moment of exposure.\n\n'Swore solemn oaths by Allah' — aqsamu billahi jahda aymanihim. Literally: the utmost of their oaths. They did not merely claim loyalty; they swore to it in the strongest terms available.\n\nThat detail is what makes the scene bite. The louder the oath, the worse the discovery looks.\n\n'That they were with you' — innahum lama'akum. With you. The word covers everything: same side, same cause, same risk.\n\nThen the believers turn to each other and ask the question. Not to the hypocrites — about them. The Quran stages it as a moment of collective recognition, and there is something quietly devastating in that.\n\n'Their deeds have been in vain' — habitat a'maluhum. 'Hubut' is used of an animal that eats until it swells and dies. The deeds looked like substance and had nothing in them.\n\n'So they have become losers' — fa asbahu khasirin. The same construction used of Cain in 5:30: fa asbaha mina al-khasirin. He became a loser.\n\nThe Quran uses one verdict for the man who murdered his brother out of envy and for the man who swore false loyalty out of fear. Both spent everything they had on a gain that evaporated.",
  "54": "'O believers! Whoever among you abandons their faith, Allah will replace them with others who love Him and are loved by Him. They will be humble with the believers but firm towards the disbelievers, struggling in the Way of Allah; fearing no blame from anyone. This is the favour of Allah. He grants it to whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.' (Quran 5:54). The answer to everyone hedging their position.\n\n'Allah will replace them' — fa sawfa ya'ti Allahu bi qawmin. The same warning given in 4:133 and 47:38. The community is not indispensable.\n\nThen four qualities of the replacements, and they are worth taking one at a time.\n\n'Who love Him and are loved by Him' — yuhibbuhum wa yuhibbunah. His love is mentioned first. The relationship begins from His side.\n\n'Humble with the believers but firm towards the disbelievers' — adhillatin 'ala al-mu'minina a'izzatin 'ala al-kafirin. Two words from opposite roots. Gentle, lowered, soft with their own; unyielding with those set against them. Most people manage the reverse — hard on their brothers, accommodating to those they want to impress.\n\n'Struggling in the Way of Allah' — the effort of 5:35.\n\n'Fearing no blame from anyone' — wa la yakhafuna lawmata la'im. This is the direct cure for the disease of 5:52. The hypocrites acted out of fear of how things would look. These people are described by the absence of exactly that fear.\n\n'This is the favour of Allah' — and note that it is called a favour, not an achievement.",
  "55": "'Your only guardians are Allah, His Messenger, and fellow believers— who establish prayer and pay alms-tax with humility.' (Quran 5:55). The positive statement that 5:51 was clearing space for.\n\n'Your only guardians' — innama waliyyukumu Allah. 'Innama' is the Arabic construction of exclusivity: only, none but. The prohibition of 5:51 is now answered with the alternative.\n\nThree are named, in descending order: Allah, then His Messenger, then the believers. This is the structure of 4:59 — obey Allah, obey the Messenger, and those in authority among you — with the same descending dependence.\n\nThen the believers are identified by two practices: 'who establish prayer and pay alms-tax'. Not by lineage, language or region. By what they do with their time and their money.\n\n'With humility' — wa hum raki'un. Literally: while bowing. The word for the bow in prayer.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records reports linking this phrase to a particular incident, and also the reading that it simply describes the believers as people of bowing — a description of their state rather than a reference to one event.\n\nThe plain sense serves the passage well. Your alliance belongs with the people who bow.\n\nThat is a definition of community with a very low barrier and a very high one at the same time. Anyone may join it. Nobody joins it by declaration alone.",
  "56": "'Whoever allies themselves with Allah, His Messenger, and fellow believers, then it is certainly Allah's party that will prevail.' (Quran 5:56). The promise attached to the choice in 5:55.\n\n'Allah's party' — hizb Allah. 'Hizb' means a group, a faction, a party in the plain sense of people who belong together. The Quran uses it of both sides: there is a hizb of Allah and, in 58:19, a hizb of Satan.\n\n'Will prevail' — humu al-ghalibun. The definite construction in Arabic makes it emphatic: they are the victorious ones.\n\nThis is the answer to the fear quoted in 5:52 — 'we fear a turn of fortune will strike us'. The hypocrites did their calculation and concluded that the safe money was elsewhere. The verse tells them their arithmetic was wrong.\n\nBut notice what the promise does not say. It does not promise that every battle is won, or that the outcome arrives on schedule. Uhud happened. The Quran is speaking about the final result, and Surah an-Nur states the same promise with its condition attached: 'Allah has promised those of you who believe and do good that He will certainly make them successors in the land... provided that they worship Me, associating nothing with Me' (Quran 24:55).\n\nSo membership of this party is not a card you carry. Verse 5:55 defined it by prayer and zakah, and 5:54 by love, humility, firmness and indifference to criticism.\n\nBelong to that, and the outcome is not your problem.",
  "57": "'O believers! Do not seek the guardianship of those given the Scripture before you and the disbelievers who have made your faith a mockery and amusement. And be mindful of Allah if you are truly believers.' (Quran 5:57). The prohibition of 5:51, now with its reason attached.\n\nLook at the qualifying clause: 'who have made your faith a mockery and amusement' — alladhina ittakhadhu dinakum huzuwan wa la'iba. This is not addressed to the People of the Book as such. It describes a specific group by a specific behaviour.\n\nThat clause is the interpretive key to this whole passage. The alliance being forbidden is with people who treat your religion as a joke, and the description does the defining rather than the label.\n\n'Huzuw' is derision; 'la'ib' is play. Together they name the attitude that does not argue with a belief but treats it as beneath argument.\n\nWhy does this rule out alliance? Because a wali is someone whose judgment you defer to and whose side you take. You cannot hand your affairs to someone who finds the basis of your life amusing.\n\nNote that the verse does not command hostility in return. It commands a limit on trust.\n\n'And be mindful of Allah if you are truly believers' — the closing condition puts the matter back where the surah keeps putting it. This is a test of whose approval you actually want, which was also the diagnosis in 5:52.",
  "58": "'When you call to prayer, they mock it in amusement. This is because they are a people without understanding.' (Quran 5:58). The mockery of 5:57, given a concrete example.\n\n'When you call to prayer' — wa idha nadaytum ila al-salah. The adhan. Five times a day, in public, a voice announcing that Allah is greatest and that success is available.\n\nThat is what they laughed at. Not a doctrine — a call.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that some would mimic the words of the adhan in mockery when they heard it.\n\n'This is because they are a people without understanding' — bi annahum qawmun la ya'qilun. The verdict is not that they are wicked but that they do not reason. 'Aql' is the faculty that ties things together — the root means to tether a camel.\n\nThe Quran diagnoses mockery as a failure of thought, which is worth sitting with. Ridicule feels like a position, and it is usually a substitute for one. It costs nothing, requires no argument, and cannot be refuted, which is precisely why it is used.\n\nThere is also an instruction implied here for the mocked. The response prescribed in this passage is not to stop calling. The adhan continued, five times a day, and it is still called today in places where those who laughed left no trace.\n\nConsistency is its own answer.",
  "59": "'Say, O Prophet, \"O People of the Book! Do you resent us only because we believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us and what was revealed before—while most of you are rebellious?\"' (Quran 5:59). A question that exposes the strangeness of the objection.\n\n'Do you resent us only because we believe' — hal tanqimuna minna illa an amanna. 'Naqima' means to hold something against someone, to find fault with it.\n\nThe list that follows is the whole of the Muslim position: belief in Allah, in what was revealed to Muhammad, and in what was revealed before. And the third item is the point.\n\nMuslims are being resented by people whose scriptures Muslims affirm. That is the oddity the question is designed to surface. The disagreement is not that Islam rejects the earlier revelation; it is that Islam accepts it and then accepts more.\n\nVerse 4:150 described the opposite posture: 'We believe in some and disbelieve in others.' Here the Muslims are faulted for refusing to do that.\n\n'While most of you are rebellious' — wa anna aktharakum fasiqun. Note 'most', not all. This surah is consistently careful with proportions, and 5:66 will say plainly that 'some among them are upright'.\n\nThe takeaway is about the shape of religious hostility. It is very often not about the content of what you believe. It is about the fact that you believe it, and that your believing it implies something about the person listening.",
  "60": "'Say, O Prophet, \"Shall I inform you of those who deserve a worse punishment from Allah than the rebellious? It is those who earned Allah's condemnation and displeasure—some being reduced to apes and pigs and worshippers of false gods. These are far worse in rank and farther astray from the Right Way.\"' (Quran 5:60). An answer to people asking who is really in the wrong.\n\nThe rhetorical setup matters. Verse 5:59 asked why they resent the believers. This verse turns the question around: you are looking for the worst outcome — here is where it is actually found.\n\n'Some being reduced to apes and pigs' — the transformation referred to in 'You are already aware of those of you who broke the Sabbath. We said to them, \"Be disgraced apes!\"' (Quran 2:65) and in 7:166.\n\nCommentators including Ibn Kathir note the punishment fell on a specific group who violated the Sabbath through a legal trick, not on a whole people, and al-Tabari records the report that they did not survive as a lineage.\n\nThat precision matters, because the phrase has been abused as a general slur. The Quran ties it to an act, and the act was circumventing a law while technically keeping it.\n\n'And worshippers of false gods' — wa 'abada al-taghut. The third item, and the gravest, though it names no transformation.\n\n'Farther astray from the Right Way' — the comparison being drawn is with the 'fasiqun' of 5:59.\n\nThe lesson turns back on the reader. The Sabbath-breakers did not deny the law. They kept its wording and defeated its purpose, which is a temptation available to any religious community.",
  "61": "'When they come to you ˹believers˺ they say, \"We believe.\" But they are committed to disbelief when they enter and when they leave. And Allah knows what they hide.' (Quran 5:61). A description of a visit.\n\n'They say, \"We believe\"' — qalu amanna. The words are correct. Nothing in the statement is wrong.\n\n'They are committed to disbelief when they enter and when they leave' — wa qad dakhalu bi al-kufri wa hum qad kharaju bih. They came in carrying it and went out carrying it. Nothing was deposited, nothing was taken up.\n\nThe image is of a person passing through a room without being touched by it. They sat in the presence of revelation, heard it recited, and left in exactly the condition they arrived in.\n\nThat is a sobering picture, and its application is not limited to hypocrites in Madinah. It is possible to attend, to listen, to say the right words, and to be entirely unaffected.\n\n'And Allah knows what they hide' — wa Allahu a'lamu bima kanu yaktumun. 'Kitman' is concealment — actively keeping something covered.\n\nNote that the Quran informs the Prophet of what they conceal but does not instruct him to expose them or bar them. They kept coming; they were received.\n\nThe community was told to know, not to act on the knowledge. Judgment on hidden things stays with the One who can see them, which is exactly the principle of 5:7: 'Allah knows best what is hidden in the heart.'",
  "62": "'You see many of them racing towards sin, transgression, and consumption of forbidden gain. Evil indeed are their actions!' (Quran 5:62). Three offences, and one word about the speed.\n\n'Racing' — yusari'una. The third appearance of this verb in the surah. They raced to disbelieve in 5:41, raced for hostile alliances in 5:52, and race toward sin here.\n\nSpeed is being treated as a symptom in its own right. Not merely that they sin, but that they hurry to it, which indicates appetite rather than weakness.\n\nThe three named: 'ithm' — sin in general; ''udwan' — transgression against others, aggression; and 'akl al-suht' — consuming what is unlawfully gained.\n\nNotice the progression: an inward category, then harm to people, then money. And it is money that closes the list, as it did in 5:42.\n\nThe Quran keeps returning to unlawful earnings in this surah. It appeared in 5:42 alongside listening to falsehood, in 4:161 alongside interest, and it will appear again in 5:63.\n\n'Evil indeed are their actions' — la bi'sa ma kanu ya'malun. A blunt closing verdict, and the Arabic construction is emphatic.\n\nThe value of this verse for self-examination is the diagnostic it offers. It is not asking whether you sin. It is asking whether you move toward it quickly — whether the thing you know is wrong is also the thing you find yourself hurrying to.",
  "63": "'Why do their rabbis and scholars not forbid them from saying what is sinful and consuming what is unlawful? Evil indeed is their inaction!' (Quran 5:63). The charge moves from the people to their leaders.\n\nVerse 5:62 condemned what they did. This verse condemns what their scholars did not do — and the Arabic makes the parallel unmistakable. Verse 5:62 ended 'la bi'sa ma kanu ya'malun', evil is what they were doing. This ends 'la bi'sa ma kanu yasna'un', evil is what they were producing.\n\nThe scholars' silence is classified as an action with a product.\n\n'Their rabbis and scholars' — al-rabbaniyyuna wa al-ahbar. Religious leadership, named specifically because leadership carries a duty that ordinary membership does not.\n\nAbu Sa'id al-Khudri reported the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, saying: 'He who amongst you sees something abominable should modify it with the help of his hand; and if he has not strength enough to do it, then he should do it with his tongue, and if he has not strength enough to do it, even then he should abhor it from his heart, and that is the least of faith' (Sahih Muslim 49a).\n\nHudhayfah ibn al-Yaman reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'By the One in Whose Hand is my soul! Either you command good and forbid evil, or Allah will soon send upon you a punishment from Him, then you will call upon Him, but He will not respond to you' (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 2169, graded hasan by Darussalam).\n\nSilence that keeps a scholar comfortable is not neutral. The Quran counts it as something manufactured.",
  "64": "'˹Some among˺ the Jews said, \"Allah is tight-fisted.\" May their fists be tied and they be condemned for what they said. Rather, He is open-handed, giving freely as He pleases...' (Quran 5:64). A slander about generosity, answered in kind.\n\n'Allah is tight-fisted' — yadu Allahi maghlulah. Literally: Allah's hand is chained. Surah Ali 'Imran records a related slur: 'Allah is poor; we are rich!' (Quran 3:181).\n\n'May their fists be tied' — ghullat aydihim. The reply uses their own image against them, which is a feature of Quranic argument.\n\n'Rather, He is open-handed' — bal yadahu mabsutatan. Both hands, outstretched. The Quran affirms the description without asking how it is to be pictured.\n\nWhy would anyone say such a thing? Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, connects it to hardship they were experiencing and to their reaction against the call to spend — the 'good loan' of 5:12 and of 'Who will lend to Allah a good loan which Allah will multiply many times over?' (Quran 2:245).\n\nThat is the psychology being exposed. A person unwilling to give often defends himself by deciding that the One asking is stingy.\n\n'Whenever they kindle the fire of war, Allah puts it out' — kullama awqadu naran lil-harbi atfa'aha Allah. A promise about plots, not about the absence of conflict.\n\n'And Allah does not like corruptors' — the verse ends with fasad, spreading ruin, which is where refusing to give eventually leads.",
  "65": "'Had the People of the Book only been faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, We would have certainly absolved them of their sins and admitted them into the Gardens of Bliss.' (Quran 5:65). After a page of charges, the offer.\n\n'Had they only been' — wa law anna. The construction of an unrealised possibility, and it is spoken with something close to regret.\n\nTwo conditions, and they are strikingly light: 'faithful and mindful' — amanu wa attaqaw. Belief and taqwa. Nothing is demanded about ancestry, and nothing about the accumulated history just described.\n\n'We would have certainly absolved them of their sins' — la kaffarna 'anhum sayyi'atihim. Kaffarah is covering over, wiping out. Everything catalogued from 5:41 to 5:64 would have been covered.\n\nThat is the point of placing this verse here. The list of failures was long and specific, and the Quran follows it immediately by saying the whole account could be settled on two conditions.\n\nThis is the pattern of the surah, and by now it is unmistakable. Verse 5:33 was followed by 5:34. Verse 5:38 was followed by 5:39. The severest passages are each followed by an opening.\n\n'The Gardens of Bliss' — jannat al-na'im.\n\nFor a Muslim reader the application is direct, because the same two conditions apply to him. A long record is not what determines the outcome. What determines it is whether a person turns.",
  "66": "'And had they observed the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been revealed to them from their Lord, they would have been overwhelmed with provisions from above and below. Some among them are upright, yet many do nothing but evil.' (Quran 5:66). A promise about this world, and a necessary correction.\n\n'Had they observed the Torah, the Gospel' — law annahum aqamu al-tawrata wa al-injil. 'Aqamu' is the same verb used for establishing prayer: to set something upright, to keep it standing.\n\n'They would have been overwhelmed with provisions from above and below' — la akalu min fawqihim wa min tahti arjulihim. Literally: they would have eaten from above them and from beneath their feet. Rain and harvest.\n\nThe Quran makes the same connection elsewhere: 'Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful of Allah, We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth' (Quran 7:96).\n\nSo obedience is presented as having worldly consequences, not merely postponed ones.\n\n'Some among them are upright' — minhum ummatun muqtasidah. And here is the correction the passage needed. After 5:59 to 5:64, a reader could be forgiven for concluding that the condemnation was total. It is not, and the Quran says so directly.\n\n'Muqtasid' means balanced, moderate, keeping to the middle. Surah Ali 'Imran is more explicit still: 'Some of them are faithful, but most are rebellious' (Quran 3:110).\n\nNever let a criticism of a group become a claim about every individual in it. The Quran will not do it, even where it is most severe.",
  "67": "'O Messenger! Convey everything revealed to you from your Lord. If you do not, then you have not delivered His message. Allah will certainly protect you from the people. Indeed, Allah does not guide the people who disbelieve.' (Quran 5:67). A command to withhold nothing.\n\n'Convey everything revealed to you' — balligh ma unzila ilayka min rabbik. And then the consequence, stated with unusual severity: withholding any part of it means the mission itself is undelivered.\n\n'Aisha said: 'Whoever tells you that the Prophet concealed something of the Divine Inspiration, do not believe him, for Allah said: \"O Messenger! Proclaim the Message which has been sent down to you from your Lord, and if you do it not, then you have not conveyed His Message\"' (Sahih al-Bukhari 7531). She used this verse as the proof that nothing was held back.\n\nThat settles an important question. There is no hidden portion of the revelation reserved for an inner circle. The whole of it was announced in public.\n\n'Allah will certainly protect you from the people' — wa Allahu ya'simuka mina al-nas. 'Aisha reported: 'The Prophet was being guarded until this verse was revealed. So the Messenger of Allah stuck his head out from the room and said: \"O you people! Go away, for Allah shall protect me\"' (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3046, which Abu 'Isa graded gharib).\n\nThe guard was dismissed the day the promise came.\n\nWhat connects the two halves of the verse is fear. The reason a message gets softened is that the messenger is afraid of the room, and the Quran removes the reason before it removes the excuse.",
  "68": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"O People of the Book! You have nothing to stand on unless you observe the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been revealed to you from your Lord.\" And your Lord's revelation to you ˹O Prophet˺ will only cause many of them to increase in wickedness and disbelief. So do not grieve for the people who disbelieve.' (Quran 5:68). The claim to religious standing, tested.\n\n'You have nothing to stand on' — lastum 'ala shay'. Literally: you are not upon anything. The image is of a person with no ground beneath him.\n\n'Unless you observe the Torah, the Gospel' — hatta tuqimu. Same verb as 5:66: keep it upright, put it into practice. Possession of a scripture confers no standing; establishing it does.\n\nThat principle applies to Muslims with equal force, and the Quran's method throughout this surah invites the transfer. A person who owns a Quran, and can recite it, and does not live by it, is described by this verse.\n\n'Will only cause many of them to increase in wickedness and disbelief' — the same clause appeared in 5:64. Revelation is presented as something that sorts people. It does not produce a neutral effect on anyone who hears it clearly.\n\n'So do not grieve for the people who disbelieve' — fa la ta'sa. The same instruction Musa received in 5:26 about a people who refused: 'do not grieve for the rebellious people'.\n\nTwo prophets, separated by centuries, given the same relief. The message was delivered; the response is not the messenger's burden to carry.",
  "69": "'Indeed, the believers, Jews, Sabians and Christians—whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.' (Quran 5:69). The criterion, stated without regard to labels.\n\nThis verse appears in near-identical form in 2:62 and in 22:17. Its repetition is deliberate.\n\nFour communities are named, and then the sentence pivots on a condition that has nothing to do with the naming: 'whoever truly believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good' — man amana billahi wa al-yawmi al-akhiri wa 'amila saliha.\n\nThe structure is the argument. The labels are listed and then made irrelevant by the criterion that follows them.\n\nThis must be read alongside the Quran's own statements about the final revelation — 3:19, 3:85, and this surah's 5:48 naming the Quran as 'muhaymin', the authority over what came before. Commentators including Ibn Kathir hold that the criterion here is met, after the coming of the Prophet, by following him, since believing in Allah includes accepting the Messenger He sent.\n\nWhat the verse forecloses is the assumption addressed in 5:18: that a community's name secures its outcome. Verse 4:123 said the same to Muslims first.\n\n'No fear for them, nor will they grieve' — la khawfun 'alayhim wa la hum yahzanun. Fear looks forward, grief looks back. Both are removed.",
  "70": "'Indeed, We took a covenant from the Children of Israel and sent them messengers. Whenever a messenger came to them with what they did not desire, they denied some and killed others.' (Quran 5:70). The pattern named in a single sentence.\n\n'Whenever a messenger came to them with what they did not desire' — bima la tahwa anfusuhum. 'Hawa' again — the appetite of the self. The trigger for rejection is identified precisely: the message conflicted with what they wanted.\n\nNot with what they knew. Not with their evidence. With their desire.\n\nThat is the same diagnosis given in 5:49, where the Prophet was warned not to follow 'their desires', and in 4:135, where injustice was traced to hawa rather than ignorance.\n\n'They denied some and killed others' — fariqan kadhdhabu wa fariqan yaqtulun. Two responses to the same problem, and the Quran notes that the second was reached when the first was insufficient.\n\nSurah al-Baqarah puts the question directly: 'Why is it that every time a messenger comes to you with something you do not like, you become arrogant, rejecting some and killing others?' (Quran 2:87).\n\nNote the grammar in the Arabic here: the denying is past tense, the killing is present. Commentators have read this as indicating that the practice continued into the present of the audience being addressed.\n\nThe verse opens with the covenant, which is where this surah began in 5:1. Every failure catalogued since has been a version of the same thing: an agreement made, and then set aside when it asked for something unwelcome.",
  "71": "'They thought there would be no consequences, so they turned a blind eye and a deaf ear. Yet Allah turned to them in forgiveness ˹after their repentance˺, but again many became blind and deaf. And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do.' (Quran 5:71). A cycle, described in one sentence.\n\n'They thought there would be no consequences' — wa hasibu alla takuna fitnah. 'Fitnah' here means trial or affliction. They calculated that nothing would follow.\n\nThat assumption is the root of the behaviour. Blindness and deafness are voluntary in this verse — 'fa 'amu wa sammu', they made themselves blind and deaf — and a person only chooses not to see when he has decided there is nothing coming.\n\n'Yet Allah turned to them in forgiveness' — thumma taba Allahu 'alayhim. The middle of the verse. After the deliberate blindness, forgiveness.\n\n'But again many became blind and deaf' — thumma 'amu wa sammu kathirun minhum. And then the same thing, again.\n\nThe structure of the sentence is the lesson: failure, mercy, failure. This is not a story about a punishment. It is a story about how a reprieve gets used.\n\nNote 'many of them' — kathirun minhum. Not all. The Quran preserves the exception even mid-condemnation, as it did in 5:66.\n\nThe uncomfortable question this leaves is about our own reprieves. Everyone has been forgiven something and returned to it. What the verse identifies as the cause is the quiet belief that consequences are theoretical.",
  "72": "'Those who say, \"Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,\" have certainly fallen into disbelief. The Messiah ˹himself˺ said, \"O Children of Israel! Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord.\" Whoever associates others with Allah ˹in worship˺ will surely be forbidden Paradise by Allah. Their home will be the Fire. And the wrongdoers will have no helpers.' (Quran 5:72). The claim answered by the person it concerns.\n\nThe argument is not made from outside. It is made by quoting 'Isa himself: 'Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord.'\n\n'My Lord and your Lord' — rabbi wa rabbakum. He places himself on the same side of the line as his audience. The Quran records the same words in 19:36, 43:64 and 3:51 — four times, in nearly identical form.\n\nVerse 5:17 argued from his vulnerability; this argues from his own testimony. Different evidence, same conclusion.\n\n'Whoever associates others with Allah will surely be forbidden Paradise' — the ruling on shirk, stated as absolutely as 4:48 states it: 'Allah does not forgive associating others with Him in worship, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills.'\n\n'And the wrongdoers will have no helpers' — the closing phrase of many verses in this surah, and it answers directly the hope that some intercessor will intervene.\n\nWhat makes this passage remarkable is its restraint. The Quran states a profound disagreement with Christian belief while calling 'Isa the Messiah in the same sentence, and while 5:82 will praise Christians for humility and 5:83 will describe their tears.\n\nDisagreement without contempt is the register the whole surah maintains.",
  "73": "'Those who say, \"Allah is one in a Trinity,\" have certainly fallen into disbelief. There is only One God. If they do not stop saying this, those who disbelieve among them will be afflicted with a painful punishment.' (Quran 5:73). The second formula, addressed after the first.\n\n'Allah is one in a Trinity' — inna Allaha thalithu thalathah. Literally: Allah is the third of three.\n\nNote that 5:72 addressed one claim — that Allah is the Messiah — and this addresses a different one. The Quran distinguishes between the formulations rather than lumping them together, which is a mark of how carefully this passage is constructed.\n\n'There is only One God' — wa ma min ilahin illa ilahun wahid. The reply is not an argument but a statement, and it is the same statement made in Surah al-Ikhlas: 'He is Allah—One and Indivisible' (Quran 112:1).\n\n'If they do not stop saying this' — wa in lam yantahu 'amma yaquluna. A warning with a condition, which means a way out is being held open.\n\n'Those who disbelieve among them' — alladhina kafaru minhum. Read that qualifier. Not all of them; those among them who reject. The Quran again refuses the total statement, exactly as in 5:66 and 5:71.\n\nEvery one of these severe verses has a limiting clause somewhere in it, and the clauses are not decorative. They are what stops a text about theology from becoming a text about people.\n\nAnd the next verse makes that explicit.",
  "74": "'Will they not turn to Allah in repentance and seek His forgiveness? And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 5:74). The door, opened one verse after the gravest charge in the surah.\n\n'Will they not turn' — a fa la yatubuna ila Allah. It is a question, not a command. There is something almost pleading in the form.\n\nConsider where this sits. Verse 5:72 said whoever commits shirk is forbidden Paradise. Verse 5:73 warned of a painful punishment. And then this.\n\nThat is the answer to anyone who thinks the Quran's position on shirk closes the matter permanently. It closes it for a person who dies on it, which is exactly what 4:48 specifies. While he lives, the invitation stands.\n\n'And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' — wa Allahu Ghafurun Rahim. The two names are placed immediately after the sin the Quran describes as unforgivable, which is not a contradiction but a definition: unforgivable if unabandoned.\n\nThe Quran states the principle without limit elsewhere: 'O My servants who have exceeded the limits against their souls! Do not lose hope in Allah's mercy, for Allah certainly forgives all sins' (Quran 39:53).\n\nBy now the reader should recognise this rhythm. Verse 5:33 then 5:34. Verse 5:38 then 5:39. Verse 5:64 then 5:65. And now 5:73 then 5:74.\n\nThe surah has never once left a condemnation standing without an exit beside it.",
  "75": "'The Messiah, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger. ˹Many˺ messengers had ˹come and˺ gone before him. His mother was a woman of truth. They both ate food. See how We make the signs clear to them, yet see how they are deluded ˹from the truth˺!' (Quran 5:75). An argument from an ordinary detail.\n\n'They both ate food' — kana ya'kulani al-ta'am. Of all the possible arguments, the Quran chooses this one.\n\nIt is chosen because it is undeniable and because of what it implies. Eating means needing. A being that requires food is a being that depends on something outside itself, and dependence is the one thing the divine cannot have. Surah al-Ikhlas names Allah 'al-Samad' — the Sustainer needed by all, who needs none (Quran 112:2).\n\nThe argument works without any scripture, any history, any dispute over texts. It rests on a fact both sides accept.\n\n'His mother was a woman of truth' — siddiqah. This is a title of the highest rank, and the Quran gives it to Maryam here while denying her divinity. Honour and worship are being carefully separated.\n\n'Messengers had come and gone before him' — qad khalat min qablihi al-rusul. He belongs to a series. Nothing in his role was unprecedented.\n\n'See how We make the signs clear to them, yet see how they are deluded' — the double 'see' — unzur... thumma unzur — invites the reader to look twice: at the clarity of the evidence, and at the persistence of the error.\n\nClarity does not compel. It only removes the excuse.",
  "76": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"How can you worship besides Allah those who can neither harm nor benefit you? And Allah ˹alone˺ is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\"' (Quran 5:76). The test applied to every object of worship.\n\n'Neither harm nor benefit' — la yamliku lakum darran wa la naf'a. The verb is 'yamliku' — possesses, controls. Not merely that they do not harm or benefit, but that they have no power to.\n\nThis is the Quran's standard criterion, and it is deliberately practical. Whatever you turn to in need, ask what it actually controls. The same argument appears against idols in 25:3 and, with striking bluntness, in 22:73: 'those idols you invoke besides Allah can never create so much as a fly'.\n\nApplied here, the point is that appealing to any created being — however honoured — is appealing to something that holds nothing.\n\n'And Allah alone is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing' — al-Sami'u al-'Alim. Notice which two names close the verse. Not the All-Powerful, which the argument might lead you to expect.\n\nHearing and knowing are the names that matter to someone praying. The reason people appeal to intermediaries is a suspicion that the direct route is not attended to — that the request needs to be carried by someone closer.\n\nThe verse answers that fear rather than the theology. He hears you. He knows what you mean.\n\nWhich makes any intermediary not merely powerless, as the first half said, but unnecessary.",
  "77": "'Say, \"O People of the Book! Do not go to extremes in your faith beyond the truth, nor follow the vain desires of those who went astray before ˹you˺. They misled many and strayed from the Right Way.\"' (Quran 5:77). The diagnosis behind the whole passage.\n\n'Do not go to extremes' — la taghlu fi dinikum. 'Ghuluww' is exceeding the limit. The same command appears in 4:171, and both times it concerns excessive praise.\n\nThat is worth noting carefully. The error being corrected is not too little religion. It is too much of the wrong kind — devotion that overshoots and ends up somewhere it was never meant to go.\n\n'Beyond the truth' — ghayra al-haqq. The limit is not arbitrary. It is set by what is true, and exaggerated love does not become true by being sincere.\n\n'Nor follow the vain desires of those who went astray before you' — the error is described as inherited. People rarely invent their extremes; they receive them.\n\n'They misled many and strayed from the Right Way' — the damage is counted twice: they went wrong themselves, and they took others with them. That doubling matters because it is what turns a private error into the thing 5:63 condemned scholars for permitting.\n\nEvery religious community faces this temptation, and Muslims were warned about it explicitly. 'Umar reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Do not exaggerate in praising me as the Christians praised the son of Mary, for I am only a slave. So call me the slave of Allah and His Messenger' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3445).",
  "78": "'The disbelievers among the Children of Israel were condemned in the revelations of David and Jesus, son of Mary. That was for their disobedience and violations.' (Quran 5:78). A condemnation recorded in their own scriptures.\n\n'In the revelations of David and Jesus' — 'ala lisani Dawuda wa 'Isa ibn Maryam. Literally: upon the tongue of. The condemnation was pronounced by the prophets they revere.\n\nThat is the force of naming these two in particular. This is not an outside accusation; it is a verdict from within the tradition, delivered by figures whose authority is not in question.\n\n'The disbelievers among the Children of Israel' — alladhina kafaru min bani Isra'il. The qualifier again. Those who disbelieved, not the people as a whole.\n\nTwo grounds are given: 'their disobedience and violations' — bima 'asaw wa kanu ya'tadun. The first is failing to do what was commanded; the second, ''udwan', is aggression — going past the limit onto someone else's rights.\n\nBoth were already named in this surah. Disobedience runs from 5:13, and ''udwan' appeared in 5:62 among the sins they raced toward.\n\nBut the verse does not stop here. It runs directly into 5:79, and the two must be read as one sentence, because the next verse identifies the specific failure that produced the condemnation.\n\nIt is not the one most readers would predict.",
  "79": "'They did not forbid one another from doing evil. Evil indeed was what they did!' (Quran 5:79). The reason for the condemnation of 5:78.\n\nOf all the offences catalogued across this surah — broken covenants, distorted scripture, killed prophets, bribery, unlawful gain — the verse that explains the curse names this one.\n\n'They did not forbid one another' — kanu la yatanahawna. The form of the Arabic verb is reciprocal: they did not restrain each other. It describes a community that stopped correcting itself.\n\nNote the precision. It does not say they all did evil. It says they stopped telling each other to stop, which is a different failure and, according to this verse, a graver one.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, explains it as their abandoning of forbidding wrong out of complacency, and notes that the one who witnesses evil in silence shares in it.\n\n'Evil indeed was what they did' — la bi'sa ma kanu yaf'alun. The third such closing in this surah, after 5:62 and 5:63. Once for their deeds, once for their scholars' silence, and now for the community's mutual silence.\n\nThe Quran has now condemned inaction three times in seventeen verses.\n\nAbu Sa'id al-Khudri reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying that whoever sees something wrong should change it with his hand, or his tongue, or hate it in his heart, 'and that is the least of faith' (Sahih Muslim 49a). The least — but not nothing, and not optional.",
  "80": "'You see many of them taking the disbelievers as allies. Truly wicked are their misdeeds, which have earned them Allah's wrath. And they will be in everlasting torment.' (Quran 5:80). The alliance theme returns, applied to a different group.\n\n'Taking the disbelievers as allies' — yatawallawna alladhina kafaru. The same word that ran through 5:51, 5:55 and 5:57 — 'wilayah', the alliance of protection and allegiance.\n\n'Which have earned them Allah's wrath' — ma qaddamat lahum anfusuhum. Literally: what their own selves sent ahead for them. The Quran locates the cause inside the person, not in circumstances.\n\nThat phrase 'sent ahead' is worth noticing. It presents deeds as things dispatched in advance of you, waiting at the destination when you arrive.\n\n'You see many of them' — tara kathiran minhum. The qualifier once more.\n\nThere is a pattern in how this surah handles the subject of alliance. It raised it about believers in 5:51, exposed the real culprits in 5:52, gave the positive alternative in 5:55, and now names it again in another community.\n\nThe consistency is the point. This is not a rule applied to one group and excused in another. Hedging your allegiance is condemned wherever it appears, and the next verse gives the reason it happens at all.",
  "81": "'Had they believed in Allah, the Prophet, and what has been revealed to him, they would have never taken those ˹pagans˺ as allies. But most of them are rebellious.' (Quran 5:81). The cause behind the behaviour of 5:80.\n\nThe logic is conditional: real belief would have made the alliance impossible. So the alliance is evidence about the belief.\n\nThat is how the Quran usually reasons about actions. Conduct is treated as a reading on an instrument, showing the state of something that cannot be observed directly.\n\n'Believed in Allah, the Prophet, and what has been revealed to him' — three objects, and the second is the one at issue. They accepted Allah in principle. What they would not accept was the messenger in front of them, and everything else followed from that refusal.\n\nNotice how this closes the argument opened in 5:52. There, the hypocrites' hedging was traced to the fear that the Muslims might lose. Here the deeper cause is named: they hedged because they did not truly believe the promise, and they did not believe it because they did not accept the one who brought it.\n\n'But most of them are rebellious' — wa lakinna kathiran minhum fasiqun. The final qualifier of this long section — 'most', not all.\n\nAnd the reason that qualifier matters becomes clear immediately. The very next verse turns to those the exception applies to, and speaks about them with warmth.",
  "82": "'You will surely find the most bitter towards the believers to be the Jews and polytheists and the most gracious to be those who call themselves Christian. That is because there are priests and monks among them and because they are not arrogant.' (Quran 5:82). A distinction drawn with care.\n\nThe verse gives reasons, and the reasons are what it is actually about: 'because there are priests and monks among them and because they are not arrogant.'\n\nTwo causes. The presence of people devoted to worship and learning, and the absence of arrogance. Neither is a statement about ethnicity or lineage; both are descriptions of a condition a community may be in or lose.\n\n'They are not arrogant' — wa annahum la yastakbirun. 'Istikbar' — the same fault that condemned Iblis and that 4:173 named as the disqualifying quality. It is the single most consistent barrier to faith in the Quran.\n\nThe historical context supports the reading. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, connects it to the Muslims who emigrated to Abyssinia and were received by the Negus and his scholars, who wept on hearing the Quran — which is exactly what 5:83 describes next.\n\nSo this is a verse about who received the message well when it arrived, and why. It is not a permanent ranking of peoples, and the surah has already refused permanent rankings: 5:69 made the criterion belief and action, and 5:66 insisted that 'some among them are upright'.\n\nHumility opens a person. Arrogance closes him. That is the whole content of the verse.",
  "83": "'When they listen to what has been revealed to the Messenger, you see their eyes overflowing with tears for recognizing the truth. They say, \"Our Lord! We believe, so count us among the witnesses.\"' (Quran 5:83). What recognition looks like.\n\n'Their eyes overflowing with tears' — tara a'yunahum tafidu mina al-dam'. 'Tafidu' is the verb for a vessel brimming over. The response is physical and involuntary, which is why the Quran uses it as evidence.\n\n'For recognizing the truth' — mimma 'arafu mina al-haqq. The word is 'arafu' — they recognised it. Not learned, not were persuaded of. Recognition implies they were meeting something they already knew.\n\nThat is the difference between this reaction and the mockery of 5:58. The same recitation reached both audiences. One found it laughable; one found it familiar.\n\n'So count us among the witnesses' — fa aktubna ma'a al-shahidin. Write us down. They ask to be recorded, and specifically among the witnesses — the ones who testify to what they have seen.\n\nThe Quran describes the same people elsewhere: 'When it is recited to them, they declare, \"We believe in it. This is definitely the truth from our Lord. We had already submitted even before this\"' (Quran 28:53), and 'there are some among the People of the Book who truly believe' (Quran 3:199).\n\nAfter twenty verses of hard argument about Trinity, extremes and alliances, the surah pauses on a group of people crying because they recognised something. That placement is not accidental.",
  "84": "'Why should we not believe in Allah and the truth that has come to us? And we long for our Lord to include us in the company of the righteous.\"' (Quran 5:84). Their reasoning, in their own words.\n\n'Why should we not believe' — wa ma lana la nu'minu billah. The question is turned around. Everyone else in this surah has been asked to justify believing. These people ask what possible justification there could be for not believing.\n\nThat reversal is the whole content of the verse. Once something is recognised as true, refusing it requires a reason, and they cannot find one.\n\n'And the truth that has come to us' — wa ma ja'ana mina al-haqq. It came to them. They did not go looking for it, and they did not construct it. It arrived, and the only remaining question was what to do about it.\n\n'And we long' — wa natma'u. 'Tama'' is eager hope, wanting something and expecting it might be given.\n\n'To include us in the company of the righteous' — ma'a al-qawmi al-salihin. Notice what they hope for. Not a rank, not a reward, not a station. Company. To be counted among a particular group of people.\n\nThat is the same request as 5:83 — 'count us among the witnesses' — and both are requests about belonging rather than about receiving.\n\nSet this against the claim in 5:18: 'We are the children of Allah and His most beloved.' Same subject, opposite postures. One assumes the place; the other asks for it.",
  "85": "'So Allah will reward them for what they said with Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever. And that is the reward of the good-doers.' (Quran 5:85). The answer to the words spoken in 5:83 and 5:84.\n\n'For what they said' — bima qalu. The reward is tied explicitly to their statement.\n\nThat is unusual. The Quran normally attaches reward to deeds, and this surah in particular has insisted on action over claim: 5:69 made it 'believe in Allah and the Last Day and do good'; 5:18 rejected an assurance based on identity alone.\n\nSo why is speech enough here? Because of what they said and the state it came from. They said 'Our Lord! We believe, so count us among the witnesses' (Quran 5:83) with their eyes overflowing. Speech that costs something, spoken by people who were already established in another community, is not a claim. It is an act.\n\n'To stay there forever' — khalidina fiha. Permanence, offered to people who had asked only to be included.\n\n'And that is the reward of the good-doers' — al-muhsinin. The verse ends by placing them in a category rather than naming them. Whoever does what they did receives what they received.\n\nNote also what is absent. There is no requirement that they earn back the years spent in another community, no probation. They recognised the truth and said so, and the verse moves straight to the Garden.\n\nThe surah has spent seventy verses on people who inherited a covenant and squandered it. Here are people who inherited nothing and were given everything.",
  "86": "'As for those who disbelieve and reject Our signs, they will be the residents of the Hellfire.' (Quran 5:86). The other outcome, stated in a single line.\n\nThe pairing is deliberate. Verse 5:85 described one group and their reward; this describes the other. Both verses are short, and their brevity is part of the effect.\n\n'Disbelieve and reject Our signs' — kafaru wa kadhdhabu bi ayatina. Two verbs, and they are not the same. 'Kufr' is covering over or refusing; 'takdhib' is actively declaring something false.\n\nThe order matters. First the refusal inside, then the public denial. This surah has traced that sequence before: the desire comes first (5:49, 5:70), and the argument is built afterwards to defend it.\n\n'Residents of the Hellfire' — ashab al-jahim. The word 'ashab' means companions or people of a place, the same construction used for the people of the Garden. It suggests belonging rather than mere confinement.\n\nThat is a hard idea, and it is meant to be. A person ends up with the company he chose.\n\nRead against the previous verse, the contrast is not between two peoples but between two responses to one recitation. The Christians of 5:83 heard it and wept. The mockers of 5:58 heard it and laughed.\n\nThe message was identical. What differed was what each brought to it, and 5:82 already named the deciding factor: arrogance, or its absence.",
  "87": "'O believers! Do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you, and do not transgress. Indeed, Allah does not like transgressors.' (Quran 5:87). A prohibition against prohibiting.\n\nRead that again, because it is easy to skip past. The command is not to avoid what is forbidden. It is to avoid forbidding what is not.\n\n'Do not forbid the good things' — la tuharrimu tayyibat ma ahalla Allahu lakum. The verb is the same one used for Allah's own act of making something unlawful. A human being who declares a lawful thing forbidden is doing something that belongs to Allah alone.\n\n'And do not transgress' — wa la ta'tadu. Note that excess in restriction is classed as ''udwan', transgression — the same word used at 5:62 and 5:78 for aggression against others.\n\nThis lands one verse after the section on ghuluww in 5:77. Extremism in doctrine and extremism in practice are treated as the same disease.\n\nAnas reported that three men, told about the Prophet's worship, thought it too little for themselves; one vowed to pray all night forever, one to fast every day, one never to marry. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'By Allah, I am more submissive to Allah and more afraid of Him than you; yet I fast and break my fast, I do sleep and I also marry women. So he who does not follow my tradition in religion, is not from me' (Sahih al-Bukhari 5063).\n\nZeal that invents its own rules is not extra piety. This verse calls it transgression.",
  "88": "'Eat of the good, lawful things provided to you by Allah. And be mindful of Allah in Whom you believe.' (Quran 5:88). The positive form of the previous command.\n\n'Eat' — kulu. An imperative. The Quran does not merely permit enjoyment here; it instructs it.\n\n'Good, lawful' — halalan tayyiban. Two conditions, and both are required. 'Halal' is what is permitted; 'tayyib' is what is wholesome and decent. Something can be technically permitted and still not good for you, and the Quran attaches both words rather than settling for one.\n\nThe same pairing runs through the Quran: 'O believers! Eat from the good things We have provided for you. And give thanks to Allah if you truly worship Him alone' (Quran 2:172), and again at 16:114.\n\n'And be mindful of Allah' — wa ittaqu Allah. Taqwa is placed in the same sentence as eating. This is the balance the surah keeps returning to. Enjoyment without limit becomes waste; restriction without warrant becomes the transgression of 5:87. Taqwa is what holds the middle.\n\nThe limit is named directly elsewhere: 'Eat and drink, but do not waste. Surely He does not like the wasteful' (Quran 7:31).\n\n'In Whom you believe' — alladhi antum bihi mu'minun. The verse closes by reminding the believer who set the terms. He permitted, so accept the permission. He limited, so respect the limit.\n\nRefusing His gift is not more respectful than accepting it.",
  "89": "'Allah will not call you to account for your thoughtless oaths, but He will hold you accountable for deliberate oaths...' (Quran 5:89). The way out of a vow, given immediately after the warning against self-imposed prohibitions.\n\nThe placement is the first thing to notice. Verse 5:87 forbade declaring lawful things unlawful. This verse deals with the person who has already done it by swearing an oath.\n\n'Thoughtless oaths' — al-laghw fi aymanikum. Speech that runs ahead of intention. The Quran states the same principle at 2:225: 'Allah will not hold you accountable for unintentional oaths, but for what you intended in your hearts.'\n\nThen the expiation: 'to feed ten poor people from what you normally feed your own family, or to clothe them, or to free a bondsperson. But if none of this is affordable, then you must fast three days' (Quran 5:89).\n\nLook at the structure. Three options, then a fallback for anyone who can afford none of them. Nobody is trapped by a bad oath because of poverty.\n\nNote also 'from what you normally feed your own family' — min awsati ma tut'imuna ahlikum. The standard is your own table, not the cheapest thing available.\n\n'Abdur-Rahman bin Samura reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'whenever you take an oath to do something and later you find that something else is better than the first, then do the better one and make expiation for your oath' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6622).\n\nAn oath is binding, but it is not a cage.",
  "90": "'O believers! Intoxicants, gambling, idols, and drawing lots for decisions are all evil of Satan's handiwork. So shun them so you may be successful.' (Quran 5:90). Four things, listed together.\n\nThe grouping is the argument. Wine and gambling look like matters of appetite; idols and divining arrows look like matters of belief. The verse puts them in one list because they share a mechanism: each one hands over your judgement to something else.\n\n'Drawing lots for decisions' — al-azlam. Arrows drawn to decide a course of action. A way of outsourcing a choice you should make yourself, before Allah.\n\n'Evil of Satan's handiwork' — rijsun min 'amali al-shaytan. 'Rijs' is filth, and it is a strong word.\n\n'So shun them' — fa ijtanibuh. Not 'do not consume' but 'stay away from', which covers the whole circle around the thing.\n\nThe prohibition arrived in stages. An earlier verse said: 'They ask you about intoxicants and gambling. Say, \"There is great evil in both, as well as some benefit for people—but the evil outweighs the benefit\"' (Quran 2:219). Then 4:43 barred approaching prayer while intoxicated. Then this.\n\nAnas reported that he was serving date wine to his uncles when it was announced that alcohol had been prohibited; they said 'Throw it away', and he threw it away (Sahih al-Bukhari 5583).\n\nIbn 'Umar reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Every intoxicant is Khamr and every intoxicant is forbidden' (Sahih Muslim 2003a) — a rule stated by effect, not by substance.",
  "91": "'Satan's plan is to stir up hostility and hatred between you with intoxicants and gambling and to prevent you from remembering Allah and praying. Will you not then abstain?' (Quran 5:91). The reason, given after the ruling.\n\nThe Quran rarely explains its prohibitions. Here it does, and the explanation is entirely about consequences you can observe.\n\nTwo harms are named. The first is social: 'hostility and hatred between you' — al-'adawata wa al-baghda'. Drink dissolves restraint and gambling creates a loser at every table; both manufacture grievances between people who were fine before.\n\nThe second is spiritual: 'to prevent you from remembering Allah and praying' — 'an dhikri Allahi wa 'ani al-salah. Not that they make prayer sinful, but that they crowd it out. Intoxication removes the clarity prayer requires; gambling absorbs the attention prayer needs.\n\n'Satan's plan' — innama yuridu al-shaytan. The word is 'yuridu', he wants. The verse describes an intention, which reframes the whole thing. What feels like a private habit is presented as someone else's design working through you.\n\n'Will you not then abstain?' — fa hal antum muntahun. A question, and the reports say the Companions answered it out loud: we have abstained.\n\nThat is a striking way to legislate. The command was given in 5:90; the reason in the first half of this verse; and the closing is not an order but an appeal to the listener's own judgement.\n\nConviction, not just compliance.",
  "92": "'Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and beware! But if you turn away, then know that Our Messenger's duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺ clearly.' (Quran 5:92). What follows a ruling that is hard to accept.\n\n'Obey Allah and obey the Messenger' — the verb is repeated. Not 'obey Allah and the Messenger' but two commands, and the repetition establishes that the Messenger's instruction carries authority in its own right, because it comes from the same source.\n\n'And beware' — wa ihdharu. A single word, left without an object. Beware of what? The verse does not say, and the silence does the work.\n\n'But if you turn away' — fa in tawallaytum. The possibility is acknowledged rather than denied. Some will hear this and refuse.\n\n'Then know that Our Messenger's duty is only to deliver the message clearly' — al-balagh al-mubin. The Prophet is not accountable for your response. His task was transmission, and it is complete.\n\nThe same limit is set at 24:54: 'Obey Allah and obey the Messenger. But if you turn away, then he is only responsible for his duty and you are responsible for yours.'\n\nThere is a kind of relief in this for anyone who carries a message to others. Delivery is the duty. Reception is not.\n\nAnd it removes the last excuse from the listener, because the message did arrive, and it was clear, and both facts have now been placed on the record.",
  "93": "'There is no blame on those who believe and do good for what they had consumed before ˹the prohibition˺, as long as they fear Allah, have faith, and do what is good...' (Quran 5:93). A question answered before it could trouble anyone.\n\nThe question was real. Wine had been lawful for years. Companions had died in that period. What about them, and what about all the years of drinking before 5:90 arrived?\n\n'No blame' — laysa 'ala alladhina amanu wa 'amilu al-salihati junahun fima ta'imu. Nothing is owed for what was permitted at the time.\n\nThis establishes a principle the jurists took directly from the verse: no ruling applies backwards. A person is judged by the guidance available to him when he acted, not by what came later.\n\nThe verse then repeats its condition three times in slightly rising terms: fear Allah and believe and do good; then believe and do good; then become fully mindful and do good. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, reads the repetition as three stages — leaving the sin, holding to faith, and then reaching ihsan, doing what is good with the awareness that Allah sees.\n\n'For Allah loves the good-doers' — inna Allaha yuhibbu al-muhsinin. The same closing as 5:85.\n\nThe larger lesson is about anxiety over the past. Many people carry guilt for years lived before they knew better. This verse addresses exactly that person, and it settles the matter without conditions except going forward well.",
  "94": "'O believers! Allah will surely test you with game within the reach of your hands and your spears to distinguish those who fear Him in secret. Whoever transgresses from now on will suffer a painful punishment.' (Quran 5:94). A test designed to be easy to fail.\n\nThe setting is ihram, the state of pilgrimage, in which hunting is forbidden. And the verse says the game will be close — 'within the reach of your hands and your spears' — tanaluhu aydikum wa rimahukum.\n\nThat is the point. The animal is not merely present; it is easy. A test is only a test when the wrong option is available and attractive.\n\n'Those who fear Him in secret' — man yakhafuhu bi al-ghayb. This is what the whole arrangement is for. In an empty valley, nobody is watching. Whether you reach for the animal reveals whether your restraint was ever about the law at all.\n\n'Bi al-ghayb' means in the unseen — both fearing a Lord you cannot see, and obeying where no one can see you. The phrase carries both, and the situation demands both.\n\nThis connects to the opening of the surah, which announced the same rule as part of the covenant: 'You are not allowed to hunt while on pilgrimage' (Quran 5:1). What was stated there as law is here presented as an examination.\n\n'Whoever transgresses from now on' — ba'da dhalik. After the warning. The excuse of ignorance is closed before it can be used.\n\nEvery prohibition eventually becomes a private moment with no witnesses. That moment is the actual test.",
  "95": "'O believers! Do not kill game while on pilgrimage. Whoever kills game intentionally must compensate by offering its equivalence—as judged by two just men among you—to be offered at the Sacred House, or by feeding the needy, or by fasting so that they may taste the consequences of their violations...' (Quran 5:95). The rule, with its remedy.\n\n'Intentionally' — muta'ammidan. Deliberate killing is what triggers the compensation. The whole ruling turns on intention, exactly as the oath ruling did in 5:89.\n\n'Its equivalence' — mithlu ma qatala mina al-na'am. A domestic animal comparable to the one killed. And the assessment is not left to the offender: 'as judged by two just men among you' — dhawa 'adlin minkum.\n\nThat detail is easy to overlook and very characteristic of this surah. Self-assessment is removed. Two upright people decide, which is the same principle 5:8 established when it required standing firm in justice, and 5:106 will require of witnesses.\n\nThen the alternatives: feeding the needy, or fasting. The same three-tier structure as 5:89 — the wealthy give an animal, others feed, and anyone who can do neither fasts.\n\n'So that they may taste the consequences of their violations' — li yadhuqa wabala amrih. The purpose is stated plainly. The penalty exists so the offence registers.\n\n'Allah has forgiven what has been done' — 'afa Allahu 'amma salaf. The past is cleared, as in 5:93.\n\n'But those who persist will be punished by Allah' — the repeat offender is treated differently from the one who slipped.",
  "96": "'It is lawful for you to hunt and eat seafood, as a provision for you and for travellers. But hunting on land is forbidden to you while on pilgrimage. Be mindful of Allah to Whom you all will be gathered.' (Quran 5:96). The limit of the previous ruling.\n\nNotice what this verse does. Immediately after two verses of restriction, it marks where the restriction ends. Sea game stays lawful throughout.\n\n'As a provision for you and for travellers' — mata'an lakum wa lil-sayyarah. The reason is practical. Pilgrims travel, and the coastal routes and the sea were how many of them ate. The law does not create hardship it has no reason to create.\n\nThis is the same balance as 5:87 and 5:88. Restrict where Allah restricted; do not extend it one step further on your own initiative.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that a man asked the Prophet, peace be upon him, about using sea water for ablution when their drinking supply was short, and he replied: 'Its water is pure and what dies in it is lawful food' (Sunan Abi Dawud 83, graded sahih by al-Albani). Al-Shafi'i, among others, took the lawfulness of sea creatures directly from this verse and this report.\n\n'But hunting on land is forbidden to you while on pilgrimage' — the restriction is restated so the concession is not overread.\n\n'Be mindful of Allah to Whom you all will be gathered' — ilayhi tuhsharun. The verse closes not with a penalty but with a destination.\n\nRules about hunting on a journey end by naming where the journey actually goes.",
  "97": "'Allah has made the Ka'bah—the Sacred House—a sanctuary of well-being for all people, along with the sacred months, the sacrificial animals, and the ˹offerings decorated with˺ garlands...' (Quran 5:97). The reason behind all the pilgrimage rulings.\n\n'A sanctuary of well-being for all people' — qiyaman lil-nas. 'Qiyam' is what keeps something standing — its support, its stability. The House is described as a structural support for human life.\n\nThat is a large claim about a building. It is explained by what the sanctuary did: in a peninsula of continuous raiding, there was one place and four months of the year in which a person was safe. Trade, worship and travel were possible because violence had a boundary.\n\nThe four items listed here are the four protections: the House, the sacred months, the offerings, and the garlands that marked an animal as consecrated so that no one would touch it or its owner.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying on the day Mecca was conquered: 'Allah has made this place a sanctuary since the creation of the heavens and the earth and will remain a sanctuary till the Day of Resurrection... Its thorns should not be uprooted and its game should not be chased' (Sahih al-Bukhari 1834).\n\nThat report explains 5:94–96. The hunting ban is not an isolated pilgrimage rule; it is one edge of a wider peace that covers even the animals.\n\n'So you may know that Allah knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth' — the sanctuary is evidence. A place where people are safe by design shows a design.",
  "98": "'Know that Allah is severe in punishment and that He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 5:98). Two statements in one short verse, and they are meant to be held together.\n\n'Severe in punishment' — shadid al-'iqab. 'All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' — Ghafurun Rahim. Neither cancels the other, and the verse gives no instruction on how to reconcile them. It simply places both in front of you.\n\nThis is the surah's whole method compressed into a line. Verse 5:33 then 5:34. Verse 5:38 then 5:39. Verse 5:73 then 5:74. Verse 5:95 spelled out a penalty and then said 'Allah has forgiven what has been done'.\n\nLiving well requires both. Fear alone produces despair, and 39:53 forbids despair. Hope alone produces carelessness, and this whole passage on hunting and oaths exists to prevent carelessness.\n\nThe order in the verse is worth noting: severity first, mercy second and at greater length — two names of mercy against one of punishment.\n\n'Know' — i'lamu. An imperative addressed to the reader. Not 'Allah is', but 'know that Allah is'. You are being told to carry this knowledge, not just receive it.\n\nAnd it closes the legal section deliberately. Everything from 5:87 to 5:97 has been rulings — food, oaths, wine, hunting, sanctuary. Rules can harden a person into a legalist or crush him into a defeatist.\n\nThis verse is placed at the end so that neither happens.",
  "99": "'The Messenger's duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺. And Allah ˹fully˺ knows what you reveal and what you conceal.' (Quran 5:99). The same limit stated at 5:92, now paired with something else.\n\nThe first half releases the Prophet from responsibility for your response. The second half makes clear that the responsibility has not disappeared — it has moved.\n\n'What you reveal and what you conceal' — ma tubduna wa ma taktumun. Two categories, and the second is the one that matters here. Delivery reaches the outside of a person. Knowledge reaches the inside.\n\nPut the two halves together and the arrangement is complete. The messenger sees what you show. Allah sees what you show and what you hide. Nothing about you is unobserved, and the part the messenger cannot reach is the part that decides the outcome.\n\nThis closes the section on wine, oaths and hunting for a reason. Verse 5:94 already made the point about hunting: the test was 'to distinguish those who fear Him in secret'. The rules can be kept outwardly by anyone.\n\nThere is also a warning to the community here. A person can be fully compliant in public and hold something entirely different inside, and the surah has described that person at length in 5:41 and 5:52.\n\nThe Prophet was not given access to hearts, and did not need it. He delivered.\n\nWhat you did with the delivery, inside, is between you and the One who already knows.",
  "100": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Good and evil are not equal, though you may be dazzled by the abundance of evil. So be mindful of Allah, O people of reason, so you may be successful.\"' (Quran 5:100). An answer to the argument from numbers.\n\n'Good and evil are not equal' — la yastawi al-khabithu wa al-tayyib. Note the words. 'Tayyib' is the same term used in 5:88 for wholesome food, and 'khabith' is its opposite: foul, rotten. The verse is not comparing quantities but qualities.\n\n'Though you may be dazzled by the abundance of evil' — wa law a'jabaka kathratu al-khabith. 'A'jaba' is to impress, to make something look attractive. And the thing doing the impressing is 'kathrah', sheer number.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's most useful observations about how people actually reason. Most arguments for doing wrong are arguments from prevalence. Everyone does it. This is normal now. You would be the only one who doesn't.\n\nThe verse does not argue against that. It simply refuses the premise. Quantity is not a measure of worth, and a majority in the wrong is still in the wrong.\n\n'O people of reason' — ya uli al-albab. The address is to those who think. Counting is not thinking.\n\n'So you may be successful' — la'allakum tuflihun. The same closing as 5:90, which had just banned wine and gambling — practices that were, at the time, everywhere.\n\nThat is the pairing. Verse 5:90 gave the ruling; this verse answers the objection that everybody was doing it.",
  "101": "'O believers! Do not ask about any matter which, if made clear to you, may disturb you...' (Quran 5:101). A limit on questions.\n\nThe context is specific. Ibn 'Abbas reported: 'Some people were asking Allah's Messenger questions mockingly. A man would say, \"Who is my father?\" Another man whose she-camel had gone astray would say, \"Where is my she-camel?\" So Allah revealed this Verse' (Sahih al-Bukhari 4622).\n\nSo the questions being forbidden are of two kinds: questions designed to embarrass, and questions whose answers a person genuinely would not want.\n\n'May disturb you' — tasu'kum. It may harm or grieve you. Some answers cost more than the curiosity was worth.\n\n'But if you inquire about what is being revealed in the Quran, it will be made clear to you' — the exception is stated immediately. Asking to learn is not what is being restricted. Verse 5:4 opens with a question about food, and it was answered.\n\nThere is a second concern, and it is legal. Every additional question invites an additional ruling, and a community can be burdened by rulings it created for itself by asking.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Leave me as I leave you, for the people who were before you were ruined because of their questions and their differences over their prophets. So, if I forbid you to do something, then keep away from it. And if I order you to do something, then do of it as much as you can' (Sahih al-Bukhari 7288).\n\nSilence in the law is often mercy, and 5:101 ends by calling it that: 'Allah has forgiven what was done'.",
  "102": "'Some people before you asked such questions then denied their answers.' (Quran 5:102). The precedent behind the warning.\n\n'Then denied their answers' — thumma asbahu biha kafirin. They asked, they were answered, and then they rejected what they were given.\n\nThat sequence is the danger. The problem was never the question. It was that the question was not asked in order to obey.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, connects it to the cow of the Children of Israel in Surah al-Baqarah, and the connection is exact. They were told 'Allah commands you to sacrifice a cow' (Quran 2:67). Instead of doing it, they asked what type. They were told: neither old nor young. They asked its colour. They asked again. Each answer narrowed the requirement until almost no animal would qualify, and when they finally found it, 'they still slaughtered it hesitantly' (Quran 2:71).\n\nEvery question they asked made the command harder, and they were the ones who made it harder.\n\nThat is what 5:101 is protecting the Muslim community from. A simple instruction had been given. Asking for detail after detail is not devotion; in that story it was a strategy for delay.\n\nThe verse says these people ended up disbelieving in the very answers they had demanded. Which reveals the motive: they never wanted the information. They wanted the argument.\n\nThe test of a question is whether you intend to act on the answer.",
  "103": "'Allah has never ordained the ˹so-called˺ baḥîrah, sâ'ibah, waṣîlah, and ḥâm camels. But the disbelievers just fabricate lies about Allah, and most of them lack understanding.' (Quran 5:103). Four invented rules, named and cancelled.\n\nSa'id bin Al-Musaiyab explained them: 'Bahira is a she-camel whose milk is kept for the idols and nobody is allowed to milk it; Sa'iba was the she-camel which they used to set free for their gods and nothing was allowed to be carried on it'; wasilah and ham were further categories of animal released from all use and dedicated to the idols (Sahih al-Bukhari 4623).\n\nLook at what these customs actually did. They took useful animals out of use. Milk that could feed a family was poured away. A camel that could carry a load carried nothing. All of it in the name of religion, and none of it commanded.\n\nThis is 5:87 illustrated: 'Do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you.' The verse warned against inventing prohibitions; this verse shows a society that had been doing it for generations.\n\n'Fabricate lies about Allah' — yaftaruna 'ala Allahi al-kadhib. That is how the Quran classifies a self-invented religious rule. Not as harmless extra caution, but as speech attributed to Allah that He did not say.\n\n'And most of them lack understanding' — akthauruhum la ya'qilun. They did not reason it through. They inherited it.\n\nWhich is exactly what the next verse addresses.",
  "104": "'When it is said to them, \"Come to Allah's revelations and to the Messenger,\" they reply, \"What we found our forefathers practicing is good enough for us.\" ˹Would they still do so,˺ even if their forefathers had absolutely no knowledge or guidance?' (Quran 5:104). The reasoning behind the invented rules of 5:103.\n\n'What we found our forefathers practicing' — ma wajadna 'alayhi aba'ana. Not an argument, a location. This is where we found ourselves.\n\nThe Quran records this answer more than once, almost word for word: 'When it is said to them, \"Follow what Allah has revealed,\" they reply, \"No! We only follow what we found our forefathers practicing\"' (Quran 2:170).\n\nThe reply is a single question: even if those forefathers knew nothing and had no guidance? The Quran does not attack the forefathers. It asks what their practice was based on, and points out that the question has never been raised.\n\nThat is the flaw in the position. Inherited practice may be right or wrong; inheritance itself tells you nothing about which. A person who follows it without asking has not chosen anything, and is therefore staking his whole standing before Allah on an accident of birth.\n\nNotice the invitation being refused: 'Come to Allah's revelations and to the Messenger' — a live source, available for questioning, against a dead one that cannot be questioned at all.\n\nThe Quran does not despise tradition. Much of this surah is about a covenant handed down through generations. What it rejects is tradition used as a reason to stop looking.",
  "105": "'O believers! You are accountable only for yourselves. It will not harm you if someone chooses to deviate—as long as you are ˹rightly˺ guided. To Allah you will all return, and He will inform you of what you used to do.' (Quran 5:105). A verse frequently misread, and corrected early.\n\nRead alone, it sounds like permission to ignore what others do. Abu Bakr addressed exactly that reading in public: 'You people recite this verse \"You who believe, care for yourselves; he who goes astray cannot harm you when you are rightly-guided,\" and put it in its improper place.' He then reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'When the people see a wrongdoer and do not prevent him, Allah will soon punish them all' (Sunan Abi Dawud 4338, graded sahih by al-Albani).\n\nThe correction turns on the condition in the verse itself: 'as long as you are rightly guided' — idha ihtadaytum. Being rightly guided includes forbidding wrong, which 5:79 has already made the decisive issue and which 3:104 states as a communal duty.\n\nSo the verse is not a licence for silence. It is a comfort for the person who has spoken and not been heard.\n\nThat person is real, and this surah is full of him. Nuh, Musa, 'Isa, and the Prophet himself delivered messages that were rejected, and 5:92 and 5:99 both said the same thing: delivery is the duty.\n\n'He will inform you of what you used to do' — 'amma kuntum ta'malun. Your file, not theirs.\n\nDo the work, then release the outcome. That is the verse.",
  "106": "'O believers! When death approaches any of you, call upon two just Muslim men to witness as you make a bequest; otherwise, two non-Muslims if you are afflicted with death while on a journey...' (Quran 5:106). A procedure for the most exposed moment there is.\n\nConsider the situation. A person is dying far from home. His property is with him, his family is not, and the only people present are strangers who could simply take it and report nothing.\n\n'Two just Muslim men' — dhawa 'adlin minkum. The same standard as 5:95, which required two upright men to assess the hunting penalty. Justice, in this surah, is repeatedly built out of the same part: witnesses of known integrity.\n\n'Otherwise, two non-Muslims if you are afflicted with death while on a journey' — the practical concession. If no Muslims are available, the testimony of others is accepted. The rule bends to reality rather than leaving the dying man without recourse.\n\nThen the safeguard: 'If you doubt their testimony, keep them after prayer and let them testify under oath.' Detained until after the prayer, when a congregation is present and the moment is weighty.\n\nAnd the oath is given in full: 'By Allah! We would never sell our testimony for any price, even in favour of a close relative, nor withhold the testimony of Allah.'\n\nTwo temptations are named — money, and family loyalty. Both are the same temptations 4:135 warned witnesses about.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that it was revealed concerning a Muslim who died while travelling with two Christian companions who kept part of his property, which was later discovered.",
  "107": "'If they are found guilty ˹of false testimony˺, let the deceased's two closest heirs affected by the bequest replace the witnesses and testify under oath ˹saying˺, \"By Allah! Our testimony is truer than theirs. We have not transgressed. Otherwise, we would surely be wrongdoers.\"' (Quran 5:107). The remedy when the oath was a lie.\n\nThe procedure has a second stage. If evidence emerges that the witnesses concealed something, the case does not end with their word. Two of the heirs may swear against them.\n\nNote who is chosen: 'the deceased's two closest heirs affected by the bequest' — al-awlayani. Those with standing in the matter, who know the property and have reason to pursue it.\n\nThis is a careful piece of law. The first oath is not treated as final, and the second is not accepted casually either — it is a sworn statement with the same weight and the same consequence for lying.\n\n'Our testimony is truer than theirs. We have not transgressed' — ma i'tadayna. The same word for transgression that ran through 5:78 and 5:87. Even the party in the right must swear that they have not overreached in claiming it.\n\nThat detail is worth pausing on. The heirs have been wronged, and the Quran still requires them to affirm that they will not go beyond their due. A grievance does not license excess.\n\n'Otherwise, we would surely be wrongdoers' — the oath includes its own condemnation if false, exactly as the witnesses' oath did in 5:106.\n\nBoth sides are bound by the same formula. Nobody is trusted merely because they are the injured party.",
  "108": "'In this way it is more likely that witnesses will give true testimony or else fear that their oaths could be refuted by those of the heirs. Be mindful of Allah and obey. For Allah does not guide the rebellious people.' (Quran 5:108). The reasoning behind the procedure.\n\nThe Quran explains its own law here, as it did with wine at 5:91.\n\n'It is more likely' — adna an ya'tu bi al-shahadati 'ala wajhiha. Closer to their giving testimony as it truly is. The word is 'more likely', not 'certain', which is an honest description of what any procedure can achieve.\n\nTwo motives are named, and they are placed in order. First, that they will tell the truth. Second, that they will 'fear that their oaths could be refuted' — an turadda aymanun ba'da aymanihim.\n\nThe second is fear of exposure, and the verse states it openly as part of the design. A system that relies only on people's conscience will be robbed by those who have none. Knowing that a lie can be challenged and overturned keeps honest the people whom conscience alone would not.\n\n'Be mindful of Allah and obey' — wa ittaqu Allaha wa isma'u. Taqwa is added at the end, because procedure is the floor, not the ceiling. The mechanism catches some liars. Awareness of Allah catches the rest.\n\n'For Allah does not guide the rebellious people' — al-qawm al-fasiqin. The same closing as 5:81.\n\nA person who wants a way around the law will find one. The law is for everyone else.",
  "109": "'˹Consider˺ the Day Allah will gather the messengers and say, \"What response did you receive?\" They will reply, \"We have no knowledge ˹compared to You˺! You ˹alone˺ are indeed the Knower of all unseen.\"' (Quran 5:109). The scene the whole surah has been building toward.\n\n'What response did you receive?' — madha ujibtum. The messengers are questioned first. The Quran states this principle directly: 'We will surely question those who received messengers and We will question the messengers themselves' (Quran 7:6).\n\n'We have no knowledge' — la 'ilma lana. This is a startling answer. These are the men who delivered the messages and watched the responses. They knew who accepted and who refused.\n\nBut they knew only the outward. Verse 5:99 has just said that Allah 'knows what you reveal and what you conceal', and that is exactly the gap the messengers are acknowledging. They saw the reply; they could not see the heart it came from.\n\nThere is also humility in it. Standing before Allah, the most knowledgeable of creation say they know nothing, because whatever they know was given and whatever they saw was partial.\n\n'You alone are indeed the Knower of all unseen' — 'allam al-ghuyub. The phrase returns at 5:116, in 'Isa's answer, and the repetition links the two scenes deliberately.\n\nThis is placed here to introduce the closing passage of the surah. Everything from 5:110 to 5:120 happens in this setting: the Day of gathering, and a conversation in which claims made on earth are checked against what is actually known.",
  "110": "'And ˹on Judgment Day˺ Allah will say, \"O Jesus, son of Mary! Remember My favour upon you and your mother...\"' (Quran 5:110). A list of gifts, recited back to the one who received them.\n\nRead the list and notice the phrase that keeps interrupting it. He moulded a bird from clay — 'by My Will'. He breathed into it and it became a bird — 'by My Will'. He healed the blind and the lepers — 'by My Will'. He raised the dead — 'by My Will'.\n\nFour times in two lines: bi idhni. By My permission.\n\nThat repetition is the argument of the whole passage. Every miracle attributed to 'Isa is confirmed as real. The Quran denies none of them. What it does is attach each one to its source, and the attachment is made in Allah's own words on the Day when nothing can be disputed.\n\nBecause the miracles are what the claim of divinity was built on. Raising the dead in particular. The verse concedes the fact and reassigns the power.\n\n'How I supported you with the holy spirit' — bi ruh al-qudus, understood as Jibril. Support given, not possessed. The same is said at 2:87.\n\n'How I taught you writing, wisdom, the Torah, and the Gospel' — taught. A recipient, not a source.\n\n'How I prevented the Children of Israel from harming you' — even his protection was not his own doing.\n\nThis is 5:75 argued again — 'the Messiah was no more than a messenger' — but now in the form of a favour recalled rather than a doctrine stated.",
  "111": "'And how I inspired the disciples, \"Believe in Me and My messenger!\" They declared, \"We believe and bear witness that we fully submit ˹to Allah˺.\"' (Quran 5:111). The disciples' own statement of faith.\n\n'Believe in Me and My messenger' — the instruction they were given names two objects, and the order matters. Allah first, then the one He sent. That is exactly what 'Isa himself said in 5:72: 'Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord.'\n\n'We fully submit' — bi annana muslimun. The word is 'muslimun', those who submit. The Quran uses it here for the disciples of 'Isa, as it uses it for Ibrahim and for the disciples again in 3:52: 'We believe in Allah, so bear witness that we have submitted.'\n\nThat usage is deliberate and it is the Quran's consistent claim: submission to Allah is the one religion every prophet brought, and the followers of 'Isa were part of it.\n\n'How I inspired' — awhaytu ila al-hawariyyin. Their faith is presented as something given, not achieved. Even the disciples' belief is listed among the favours to 'Isa in this passage.\n\n'And bear witness' — wa ishhad. They ask to be recorded as witnesses, which is precisely what the Christians of 5:83 asked: 'count us among the witnesses'.\n\nThe surah is drawing a line between the first followers of 'Isa and the ones who wept at the Quran centuries later. Both said the same thing.\n\nThe passage now turns to a specific incident from the disciples' own history — one that gives this surah its name.",
  "112": "'˹Remember˺ when the disciples asked, \"O Jesus, son of Mary! Would your Lord be willing to send down to us a table spread with food from heaven?\" Jesus answered, \"Fear Allah if you are ˹truly˺ believers.\"' (Quran 5:112). The request that names the surah.\n\n'A table spread with food' — ma'idah. The surah is called al-Ma'idah after this word, and it appears only here and in 5:114.\n\nThe placement is remarkable. The chapter that opens with 'honour your obligations' (Quran 5:1) is named after a meal that was asked for, granted, and turned into a test.\n\n'Would your Lord be willing' — hal yastati'u rabbuka. The phrasing is what draws the rebuke. It sounds like a question about capacity, and 'Isa hears it as a question about faith.\n\n'Fear Allah if you are truly believers' — ittaqu Allaha in kuntum mu'minin. He does not answer the question. He redirects it to the state of the people asking.\n\nThat response connects this scene to 5:101. There too, a question was the problem — not because questions are forbidden, but because of what these particular questions revealed about the asker.\n\nThese are the same disciples praised one verse earlier for saying 'we fully submit'. And here they are asking for a sign.\n\nThe Quran does not present this as hypocrisy. It presents it as the ordinary condition of faith, which is real and still wants reassurance. Their next words in 5:113 will explain what they were after.",
  "113": "'They said, \"We ˹only˺ wish to eat from it to reassure our hearts, to verify you are indeed truthful to us, and to become its witnesses.\"' (Quran 5:113). The disciples explain themselves after the rebuke of 5:112.\n\nThree reasons, and they are worth separating.\n\n'To reassure our hearts' — li natma'inna qulubuna. This is not doubt about the truth; it is the wish for the heart to settle on what the mind already accepts. The Quran treats that wish as legitimate. Ibrahim asked for exactly the same thing: 'My Lord! Show me how you give life to the dead.' Allah responded, 'Do you not believe?' Ibrahim replied, 'Yes I do, but just so my heart can be reassured' (Quran 2:260).\n\n'To verify you are indeed truthful to us' — wa na'lama an qad sadaqtana. To know by experience what they had accepted on report.\n\n'And to become its witnesses' — wa nakuna 'alayha mina al-shahidin. The same word again — shahidin — that the Christians used in 5:83 and the disciples used in 5:111.\n\nThey want to testify to something they saw rather than something they were told, because a witness who has seen can carry the message further.\n\nSo their request was not the challenge that 5:112 made it sound like. But the phrasing had been careless, and 'Isa corrected the phrasing before granting the substance.\n\nThe lesson is in the gap between the two verses. Wanting certainty is human. Asking as if Allah's ability were in question is not the way to ask for it.",
  "114": "'Jesus, son of Mary, prayed, \"O Allah, our Lord! Send us from heaven a table spread with food as a feast for us—the first and last of us—and as a sign from You. Provide for us! You are indeed the Best Provider.\"' (Quran 5:114). The request, made properly.\n\nCompare this with how the disciples asked in 5:112. They said 'Would your Lord be willing'. He says 'O Allah, our Lord' — Allahumma rabbana. He includes himself with them and addresses Allah directly.\n\nThat is the correction in practice. He did not refuse the request; he reformulated it.\n\n'A feast for us—the first and last of us' — 'idan li awwalina wa akhirina. ''Id' is a recurring celebration, a day marked and remembered. He asks not merely for food but for something that will still mean something to people who were not there.\n\n'And as a sign from You' — wa ayatan minka. He names the real purpose. Not the meal — the evidence.\n\n'Provide for us! You are indeed the Best Provider' — wa arzuqna wa anta khayru al-raziqin. He ends by naming Allah with the attribute the request depends on. This is how the Quran teaches supplication: ask through what He is.\n\nNotice also that 'Isa asks for himself too — 'provide for us'. He is among the hungry. That fits everything this surah has said about him: he ate food (5:75), he needed protection (5:110), and here he needs provision.\n\nThe one they would later call divine is shown praying for lunch.",
  "115": "'Allah answered, \"I am sending it down to you. But whoever among you denies afterwards will be subjected to a torment I have never inflicted on anyone of My creation.\"' (Quran 5:115). The answer, and the condition attached to it.\n\n'I am sending it down' — inni munazziluha 'alaykum. The request is granted in full, without hesitation.\n\nThen the warning, and it is the most severe in the surah: 'a torment I have never inflicted on anyone of My creation' — 'adhaban la u'adhdhibuhu ahadan mina al-'alamin.\n\nWhy so severe, for people who had just been praised as believers? Because of what they asked for and received.\n\nThey asked for certainty. They asked to see, so that no doubt would remain. And a person who has seen and then denies has nothing left to plead. Ignorance is gone. Ambiguity is gone. The only thing that could produce denial afterwards is deliberate refusal.\n\nThis is the principle behind 5:94 as well, where the punishment applies to 'whoever transgresses from now on' — after the warning. Knowledge raises the stakes of every subsequent choice.\n\nThere is a practical warning in this for anyone who prays for a sign. Asking to be shown is asking to be held to what you were shown.\n\nThe surah began with a covenant in 5:1 — 'honour your obligations'. Here is a covenant in miniature: a gift given, a condition stated, and full clarity on both sides.\n\nThe whole chapter has been about what people did with exactly that arrangement.",
  "116": "'And ˹on Judgment Day˺ Allah will say, \"O Jesus, son of Mary! Did you ever ask the people to worship you and your mother as gods besides Allah?\" He will answer, \"Glory be to You! How could I ever say what I had no right to say?...\"' (Quran 5:116). The question put to him directly.\n\nThis returns to the scene opened in 5:109, where the messengers were gathered and questioned.\n\nThe question is asked of 'Isa, in front of everyone, and the answer is his own. Every claim made about him in 5:17 and 5:72 is now put to the person concerned.\n\n'Glory be to You' — subhanaka. His first word is not a denial but a declaration of Allah's purity from any partner. The denial follows from it.\n\n'How could I ever say what I had no right to say?' — ma yakunu li an aqula ma laysa li bi haqq. Not merely 'I did not', but 'it was never mine to say'. He rejects the standing, not just the act.\n\n'If I had said such a thing, you would have certainly known it' — he offers Allah's own knowledge as the evidence for his innocence. There is no better witness and no other needed.\n\n'You know what is hidden within me, but I do not know what is within You' — the line that settles the matter. A being who does not know what is in Allah is not Allah.\n\n'Indeed, You alone are the Knower of all unseen' — 'allam al-ghuyub. The same phrase the messengers used in 5:109. He answers as one of them.",
  "117": "'I never told them anything except what You ordered me to say: \"Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord!\" And I was witness over them as long as I remained among them. But when You took me, You were the Witness over them —and You are a Witness over all things.' (Quran 5:117). His account of what he actually taught.\n\n'Except what You ordered me to say' — illa ma amartani bih. He claims no content of his own.\n\nAnd the message he quotes is word for word what 5:72 already reported him saying: 'Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord.' The Quran quoted him to his community; here he confirms the quotation himself.\n\n'As long as I remained among them' — ma dumtu fihim. He limits his testimony to his lifetime. What was done in his name afterwards, he does not speak to.\n\nThat limit is the heart of the answer, and the Prophet applied it to himself. Ibn 'Abbas reported him saying that on the Day of Resurrection some of his followers will be driven to the left, 'I will say, \"O my Lord! (They are) my companions!\" Then a reply will come, \"You do not know what they did after you.\" I will say as the pious slave said: \"And I was a witness over them while I dwelt amongst them. When You took me up, You were the Watcher over them\"' (Sahih al-Bukhari 4625).\n\nEvery teacher is answerable for what he taught, not for what was later built on his name.\n\n'You are a Witness over all things' — no gap in the record, on either side of his death.",
  "118": "'\"If You punish them, they belong to You after all. But if You forgive them, You are surely the Almighty, All-Wise.\"' (Quran 5:118). How he closes.\n\nThis is the last thing 'Isa says in the Quran, and it is not a verdict. Asked about people who made him into a god, he neither condemns them nor pleads for them. He submits the matter.\n\n'They belong to You after all' — fa innahum 'ibaduk. They are Your servants. Even in their error, they are His and the decision is His.\n\n'You are surely the Almighty, All-Wise' — al-'Azizu al-Hakim. Consider which names he chooses. Forgiveness would normally be paired with 'the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful', as in 5:74. Instead he pairs it with power and wisdom: if You forgive, no one can overrule You, and it will not be a lapse. Mercy would be entirely deliberate.\n\n'Abdullah b. 'Amr b. al-'As reported that the Prophet recited Ibrahim's words 'whoso followeth me, he verily is of me' (Quran 14:36) and 'Isa's words in this verse, 'then he raised his hands and said: O Lord, my Ummah, my Ummah, and wept'. Allah sent Jibril to ask why he wept, and then said: 'Verily We will please thee with regard to your Ummah and would not displease thee' (Sahih Muslim 202).\n\nThe Prophet's response to this verse was tears for his own community. That is how it was meant to be heard.",
  "119": "'Allah will declare, \"This is the Day when ˹only˺ the faithful will benefit from their faithfulness. Theirs are Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him. That is the ultimate triumph.\"' (Quran 5:119). The verdict, delivered after 'Isa steps back.\n\n'The Day when only the faithful will benefit from their faithfulness' — yanfa'u al-sadiqina sidquhum. The word is 'sidq' — truthfulness. What benefits a person is having been truthful.\n\nThat is the summary of this whole surah. It has been about the distance between what people said and what they were: hypocrites who said 'we believe' while racing to the other side (5:41, 5:52), a people who claimed to be 'the children of Allah and His most beloved' (5:18), witnesses who might swear falsely over a dying man's estate (5:106). Against them, the people who wept and said 'count us among the witnesses' (5:83), and now this.\n\nTruth benefits on that Day because on that Day it is the only thing left.\n\n'Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him' — radiya Allahu 'anhum wa radu 'anh. A two-way statement, and the second half is not obvious. They are pleased with Him, with everything He decreed, including the parts they did not understand.\n\nAbu Sa'id al-Khudri reported that Allah will ask the people of Paradise if they are pleased, and after they say they are, He will say: 'I will bestow My pleasure and contentment upon you so that I will never be angry with you after for-ever' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6549).\n\nBetter than the Garden is the One who gave it.",
  "120": "'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything within. And He is Most Capable of everything.' (Quran 5:120). The last verse of the surah.\n\nIt is the same sentence the surah has used twice before, at 5:17 and 5:18, both times to end an argument about who owns what. Here it ends the whole chapter.\n\n'The kingdom of the heavens and the earth' — mulku al-samawati wa al-ard. Ownership, not merely rule.\n\nSet against everything the surah has covered, the placement is exact. People had claimed a special status by descent (5:18). They had declared animals sacred and refused to milk them (5:103). They had forbidden lawful food (5:87), hedged their allegiance (5:51, 5:80), sworn false oaths over a dead man's property (5:106), and raised a servant of Allah to the rank of his Master (5:72).\n\nEvery one of those is a claim over something that was never theirs. Status, law, animals, property, and finally divinity itself.\n\nThe closing verse cancels them all with a single statement of ownership. Nothing in the heavens or the earth is unowned, so nothing is available to be claimed.\n\n'And He is Most Capable of everything' — wa huwa 'ala kulli shay'in qadir. Ownership with the power to enforce it.\n\nReturn now to 5:1: 'O believers! Honour your obligations.' The surah opened by asking people to keep their agreements, and closes by naming the One they made them with.\n\nEverything between the first verse and the last is the record of what happens either way."
};
